# Architecture

Go (gin) server that renders `templates/*.html` server-side; page behavior
lives in `static/scripts/*.ts`, bundled to sibling `.js` files by esbuild
(`npm run build:ts`). Handlers in `handlers/` talk to the sensor over ZMQ,
shell out to system tools, and read data dirs named by `config.ini` or by
CLI flags (`handlers/arguments.go`, `handlers/paths.go`).

- **Heartbeats are pushed as well as polled.** The controller sends one about
  every 6 s, and also the moment a recording starts or stops (ds).
  `GET /api/v1/status/stream` is a server-sent event of the same body as
  `GET /api/v1/status`, sent once on connecting and again on every heartbeat;
  the recording page listens on it and keeps its 5 s poll as the fallback.
  A gin SSE handler that writes an event before `c.Stream` has to call
  `c.Writer.Flush()` itself: `c.Stream` only flushes after its own steps, so
  otherwise the headers and that first event sit in the buffer until the next
  one. `GetPsdStream` still has that shape.
- **Most commands to the sensor are fire-and-forget; two are request/reply.**
  The shared PUB socket to the controller (5004) carries no answer, so a page
  learns the outcome only from a later heartbeat, which the controller sends
  about every 6 s. The CoT example send (`SendCotTest`, cot-xlate on 6006) and
  the record command (`PostRecording`, processing's recorder on 6009)
  instead open a fresh REQ socket per request and answer with what the
  service did, including refusals. A command whose result a person is
  waiting on belongs on that pattern; the heartbeat then only catches up.
  The sandbox's fake sensor answers on 6009 too.
- **`config.ini` is two files, and the portal only ever writes one of them.**
  The shipped default (`-config`, `/opt/ds/config.ini`) is on the read-only
  image and an image update replaces it. The overlay (`-config_overlay`,
  `/config/ds/config.ini`) holds only the keys that differ on this sensor.
  Every ds service reads both, overlay key first, and so does the portal:
  `loadConfig` merges them for every read and `setConfigValues` writes a
  change into the overlay, dropping any overlay key that goes back to the
  shipped value. The config page shows the merged view, saves through the
  same diff, downloads the merged file and takes an upload as the whole
  desired configuration, so a downloaded file round-trips. Never `ini.Load`
  the shipped path on its own or `SaveTo` either path directly.
- **Every directory the portal writes is a `[paths]` key in that merged
  file**, the same section the ds services read, so moving one is one line
  in the overlay for both sides: `state_dir` (registration, identity, SOIs,
  profiles), `ml_manifest_overlay`, `cert_dir`, `cot_template_dir`,
  `model_upload_dir`, `class_model_upload_dir`, `portal_dir` (the portal's
  own sqlite.db and TLS pair, see `PortalFile`) and `radio_plan_dir`. A flag
  overrides a key, which is how the sandbox and the UI tests point everything
  at a scratch directory. `ResolvePaths` fills the empty flags at startup; a
  key the file leaves out gets the same default the services use. The same
  rule covers what processing writes and the portal only reads: the captures,
  dwell and spectrogram directories come from `[collection] collection_path`,
  `[processing] stream_recording_path`, `automation_dwell_recording_path` and
  `[collection] frontend_jpeg_path`, and the SOI and profile files from
  `[interpreter] sois_file` and `profiles_file` when those are set. Never add
  a flag default that repeats a config.ini value.
- **`sqlite.db` is versioned, migrated forward only, and never written by an
  older portal.** `handlers/database.go`: `schema_version` is a one-row
  table, `SchemaVersion` is the newest schema the binary knows, and
  `migrations[i]` takes the file from version `i` to `i+1` in one transaction
  with the version bump, logged as a `schema:` line (one per migration on the
  first start after an upgrade, none on later starts). Version 1 is the
  pre-versioning schema (`users` + `jwt_denylist`), so a file without the
  table is recorded as 1 and migrated from there; a fresh file is created at
  `SchemaVersion`. `OpenDatabase` (called from `main.go` before the routes
  are mounted, so `SetupRoutes` no longer touches the schema) does the
  comparison. A file newer than the binary (the other slot of an A/B unit
  wrote it, since `/config/ds/portal` is shared by both slots and is never
  rolled back) is reopened with `file:<path>?mode=ro&_query_only=1`,
  `args.DBReadOnly` and the two versions are set, `GET /api/v1/status`
  carries `dbSchemaMismatch {present, fileVersion, binaryVersion}` (in the
  500 body too), and `templates/db_schema_banner.html` shows a banner on
  every page: included from `sidebar.html` and from the standalone
  `login.html`, which `GetLogin` renders with the same three keys
  (`dbSchemaTemplateKeys`). Login and every read keep working; the writes
  (`/logout`, `POST /api/v1/password`, the users routes) answer 503 naming
  the read-only database through `dbWriteFailed`, and 500 on any other
  write failure. The 503 needs both the package-level `dbReadOnly` that
  `OpenDatabase` set and a SQLITE_READONLY error: a root-owned or
  read-only-mounted file at the current version fails the same way and is
  a 500 with the error logged, not a claim about a newer version. On the
  503 path `/logout` still expires the cookie and sends a browser to
  `/login?db_read_only=1`; the token stays valid server-side until it
  expires. None of them `log.Fatal` any more, on a read either
  (`validateJWT`, `GetUsers` answer 500): a failed request must never take
  the portal down.
  The rules for a new migration: append to `migrations` and bump
  `SchemaVersion`; additive only (new tables, new nullable columns), never
  drop, rename or change the meaning of a column, because the older slot
  reads the same file after a rollback and must still find what it knew;
  never migrate down. The same additive-only rule covers the config
  overlay and the manifest overlay the portal writes.
- **Radio plans come from two directories.** The shipped set is `[sdr]
  plan_dir` on the image; plans the sensor writes (every plan saved from the
  page, since sdr writes them on the portal's behalf), `default.txt` and the
  `0.rplan` copy are in `radio_plan_dir`. The list is the union with the
  sensor's copy winning by name, a read resolves the same way, and delete
  only touches the sensor's copy: a shipped plan answers 409, and saving a
  plan under its name shadows it instead.

- **Access is a set of permissions per account, read from the users table on
  every request.** `handlers/permissions.go`: seven keys (`signals`, `tasking`,
  `models`, `network`, `system`, `users`, `system_access`), each with a label
  and what it opens, stored as a comma-separated column beside the old `role`
  column (kept and still written by `roleForPermissions` for the other OTA
  slot, never read). `admin`/`admin` is seeded with every permission and
  `user`/`user` with the standard set (signals, tasking, models,
  network); a roles-era database is migrated the same way (`migrateToV3`; version 2 is the collection insights table).
  `Authenticate` takes the username from the JWT and the permissions from the
  row, so a change on the Users page holds on the next request and a deleted
  account's token is a 401. The gates are `RequirePermission(args, key)` and
  `RequireAnyPermission`: 403 with JSON for an API call, 403 with
  `templates/forbidden.html` (sidebar included, the permission named) for a
  page. A page is hidden unless the account holds the permission that covers
  it, reads included: the SOIs page is Signals (Tasking reads the SOI list
  too, since a profile names them); plans, tasking, dwell, recording,
  profiles, orientation, collections and spectrograms are Tasking; the model
  pages and every `/api/v1/model` route are Models; network settings and the
  Connections pages (CoT, Sensor HQ, identity) are Network; Config, NetBird,
  Services, Time, Power, LEDs and the events clear are System. A detection
  bundle needs Signals and Models. What every account has: the overview,
  Events, Monitor, its token and password. Users opens the Users page, adds
  and deletes accounts and gives or takes every permission except System and
  System access; those two can only be given or taken by System access, which
  opens the page too. The last account holding both Users and System access
  cannot lose one or be deleted, and no account can delete itself.
  `GetHeaderStruct` folds each page's `*_enabled` key with its permission
  (`featureKeyPermissions`) and gives every template `can_<key>`, `username`,
  `permissions`; `/api/v1/features` reports the same
  fold plus the permissions. The Users entry is in the account menu, not
  Settings. Never gate by a string compare in a handler or by anything in the
  token: use the middleware, and tell the template through the header.
- Never edit `static/scripts/*.js` directly — they are generated from the
  `.ts` sources. Verify TS changes with `npm run typecheck:ts`.
- The API spec at `/swagger` is generated by `swag init` from the `// @Router`
  comment blocks on the handlers into the gitignored `swagger/` (`docs/` is
  the human documentation, tracked). The blocks are
  the source of truth, and `tests/backend/swagger_test.go` holds them to the
  router: every `/api/` route needs one, and every block needs a registered
  route. A route has to be a named handler to carry a block, so none goes in
  as an inline closure.
- Imports are written `./widgets/x.js` even when the source is `x.ts`, which
  TypeScript understands and esbuild takes literally: a compiled `x.js` sitting
  beside `x.ts` shadows it, and edits to the `.ts` silently do nothing. If a
  stray `tsc` emit leaves one behind, delete it. `widgets/logic_builder.js` was
  checked in beside its `.ts` for a long time and shadowed it exactly this way,
  which is how the logic builder came to be edited for a release without any of
  it shipping. There is no `.js` in `widgets/` now that has a `.ts` beside it,
  and there should never be one again.
- A containerized frontend sandbox (fake sensor, no real hardware) lives in
  `sandbox/` — see `sandbox/README.md` for usage and `.ai/frontend-sandbox.md`
  for the agent-facing rules that apply when working inside it.
- The `/system` page's "Download logs" (`GET /api/v1/system/archive?boots=`,
  `handlers/log_archive.go`) reads a journal that persists across reboots on
  the Yocto image, capped at 2G (meta-dsensos `VOLATILE_LOG_DIR = "no"`). Three
  things about it are not obvious. Boots are addressed by ID from
  `journalctl --list-boots`, never by offset: these sensors boot before their
  clock is set, and `-b -1` then names the wrong boot or none. The archive is
  staged under `/data/logs/` and removed when the request ends, not in `/tmp`,
  which is a tmpfs on that image. It is written by its own `streamLogArchive`,
  not `streamTarGz`, which emits an entry for the directory it is given: that
  entry unpacks either as the staging path or, as `""`, into `.`, where GNU tar
  applies the staging directory's 0700 to the user's own folder. Boot
  directories are named by position and end time (`boot-2-previous (ended
  2026-10-14 15.58 UTC)`), the end because a boot's first entries predate the
  clock being set. And its UI spec cannot mock the download with
  `page.route`: Chromium does not route a request started by an `<a download>`,
  so the real server answers it. `system.spec.ts` records the link the page
  clicks instead.
- **Every command leaves through `publishSysCom` (`handlers/trace.go`), and every
  request has a trace ID.** `TraceIDs` (first middleware in `SetupRoutes`) adopts
  a caller's `X-Trace-ID` or W3C `traceparent`, else mints one, puts it in the
  gin context and the request's `context.Context`, and answers with it in
  `X-Trace-ID`. `publishSysCom` copies it onto `SysCom.trace_id`, so the C++
  services log the same `trace=<id>` token for the same action; the access log
  and `logf` print it on the portal's side. A new handler that marshals a
  SysCom and calls `zmqPublish` itself sends a command nobody can follow — use
  `publishSysCom`, and pass `c.Request.Context()` to any helper that sends.
- **The event log is read here and written only by ds's eventlog service.** The
  portal opens `[eventlog] db_path` from the merged config.ini, else
  `eventlog/events.db` under `[paths] log_dir` (`/data/ds/logs`), the default
  eventlog uses too (`ResolvePaths`). It is the sensor's own record and grows
  to `max_mb`, so it sits with the logs on `/data`, not with the state on
  `/config`. The portal opens it read-only and never creates it, so the schema
  has one owner and the portal needs no migrations; a missing file is a 503,
  not an error to fix here. The portal's own events (`portal.command.sent`,
  `config.changed`, `service.started`) go to eventlog over ZMQ PUSH
  (`events.Emit`, 127.0.0.1:6010, ds `event_log_port`), never blocking a
  request. The recording command goes to processing's REQ/REP recorder rather
  than the bus, so `PostRecording` stamps the trace and calls
  `recordCommandSent` itself, as `publishSysCom` does for every other command. The log archive
  writes each boot's events as `events.ndjson` and leaves the database itself
  out of its copy of the state directory: it can be hundreds of megabytes.
- **Live events come from one reader, `EventsHub` (`handlers/events_hub.go`),
  shared by the SSE stream (`/api/v1/events/stream`, the events page) and the
  WebSocket (`/api/v1/events/ws`, the `/monitor` page; client in
  `static/scripts/widgets/event_stream.ts`).** The WebSocket is registered only
  with the `monitor` flag, like its page, so it is off on a sensor; turning the
  flag on, or moving the route out of that block, is all a new consumer needs. It reads SQLite only while someone
  is subscribed, and is woken by the heartbeat loop whenever a `SENSOR_EVENT`
  passes on the bus: eventlog republishes each row on comm_rx after the write
  commits, so the row is readable by then. The 1 s tick is only the fallback.
  A subscriber that falls 64 batches behind is cut off (SSE ends, the WebSocket
  closes with 4000) and resumes from its last seq: SSE carries the seq as its
  `id`, so `Last-Event-ID` does this without the page's help. Live rows are
  filtered in Go by `EventQuery.Matches`, which has to agree with `where()`'s
  SQL; `TestEventQueryMatchesAgreesWithSQL` holds the two together. The
  WebSocket keeps gorilla's default same-origin check on purpose: the
  handshake carries the session cookie from any site, and nothing like CORS
  stands in front of it.
- `/monitor` (`templates/monitor.html`, `static/scripts/monitor.ts`) is a minimal
  live view on that WebSocket, **sandbox only for now**: the `monitor` feature
  flag is off by default and in the shipped `config.json`, and only
  `sandbox/config.json` (and the Playwright fixture) turn it on. Off, the route
  is not registered and the sidebar hides the link; the WebSocket API itself
  follows `events`, not this flag. The page shows the newest 100 rows that match, then each new
  one at the top, 500 kept, a minimum-severity filter, and Pause, which holds
  new rows back instead of dropping them. Rows before the server's `ready` are
  the backlog and are shown even while paused. In the sandbox the fake sensor's
  eventlog (`sandbox/fakesensor/eventlog.go`) keeps it moving.
- The events page's dropdowns come from `/api/v1/events/facets`, which groups
  only the newest 50,000 rows (`facetWindowRows`): the log can hold a gigabyte,
  and the page recounts the dropdowns after every burst of new rows. Each list is
  counted with every filter but its own applied. Anything older than the window
  can still be typed into the type and trace fields.
- Clearing the log (`POST /api/v1/events/clear`, System, the events page's
  "Clear log") goes through eventlog, since the portal only reads the file: the
  portal emits `eventlog.clear_requested`, eventlog deletes every row and logs
  `eventlog.cleared` on the request's trace, and the handler waits up to
  `EventsClearWait` for that row. seq is AUTOINCREMENT, so it keeps counting
  and stream cursors carry on.
- **The event log is the customer's record; the journal is support's.** The
  trace view (`GET /api/v1/traces/{id}`, `/events#trace=<id>`, what a command
  toast links to) shows the events carrying a trace and nothing else. Events
  are written on purpose for the operator to read; journal lines are not, and
  if something a customer needs to see is only in the journal, the fix is a
  new event in the service, not a journal lookup in the portal. Support finds a
  trace's journal lines over ssh or in the log archive
  (`journalctl --since ... | grep trace=<id>`): the Yocto image's journalctl
  has no PCRE2, so `--grep` fails there with "PCRE2 support is not compiled
  in". The portal used to merge those lines into the trace view (#292) and
  never matched that message, so on a sensor the view only ever showed a
  journal error.
- Either status LED can follow a service: `PUT /api/v1/status_led` (the
  `/status_leds` page's selects) sets LED 1 or 2, and so do the `/system`
  page's "Set LED 1"/"Set LED 2" (`set-status-led` with `led`, which defaults
  to 2 for older callers). Both go through `setStatusLed` in
  `handlers/status_led.go`, which runs `sudo ds-status-led-watch set-unit <n>
  <arg>` (Jackson images only, from meta-dsensos' `ds-status-led` recipe; the
  two-argument form arrived with its "either indicator can follow a unit"
  change, and an older tool rejects it with a usage error). `<arg>` is
  `<svc>.service`, `off` (held dark), or the release word: `system` for LED 1
  (back to the monitor's boot/ready/degraded/fault) and `none` for LED 2. The
  API spells a release as an empty unit, so the tool's words never reach it as
  service names. While LED 1 follows a unit or is off, the monitor stands
  aside and degraded and fault are not shown. The tool writes its argument
  verbatim into `/config/identity/status-led/conf`, which holds only the two
  `IND<n>_WATCH_UNIT` lines, is read on top of the shipped
  `/etc/ds-status-led.conf`, and survives reboots and slot flips (meta-dsensos
  `0892d95`; older images wrote a full copy to `/run/ds-status-led/conf`,
  lost at reboot). A unit name from that file reaches a root shell. So the
  `ds-sensor-portal.sudoers` rule lists every
  allowed unit exactly, once per LED. Never widen it to `set-unit *` or
  `sdr@*`: either one lets any `%developers` user run code as root. For the
  same reason, sdr@ instances get no LED action. A service added to
  `staticServices` in `handlers/system.go` also needs a matching entry in both
  sudoers LED lines. The portal never writes that file itself: `set-unit`
  does, inside the portal's sandbox, whose `ReadWritePaths` meta-dsensos'
  hardening drop-in extends to `-/config/identity/status-led`.
  `getStatusLed` reads `IND1_WATCH_UNIT`/`IND2_WATCH_UNIT` from the shipped
  file and then from `/run/ds-status-led/conf` on top, as the watcher layers
  them. On the `/config` images that path is a tmpfiles symlink to the
  overlay, dangling until the first `set-unit`; on older ones it is the full
  runtime copy. Either way the page shows the shipped default until
  something is set, and the overlay's lines win once it is. Deleting the
  overlay goes back to the defaults; the portal has no control for that.
- `/status_leds` sets what each LED follows and is a guide to what the lights
  mean, transcribed into `static/scripts/status_leds.ts` from meta-dsensos PR
  #94 (`recipes-core/ds-status-led`). Nothing checks the two stay in step:
  change the recipe's defaults and this table is wrong. The reader picks the
  two-LED (Rev B) or three-LED (Rev A) layout, because software can't tell
  them apart: an unfitted red LED and an unlit one look the same from the
  Jetson. Each LED's guide follows its setting: LED 1 shows the device table
  on the sensor state and LED 2's service table while it follows a service,
  and an LED that is off (or LED 2 following nothing) has no guide. The
  revision A LEDs are drawn without a colour, since some are green and some
  yellow. LED 2 cannot show the sensor state: the tool refuses `set-unit 2
  system` and the monitor only paints indicator 1. Its nav entry is hidden
  unless `ds-status-led-watch` is installed, but the page still loads without
  it and says so, with the selects hidden.
- **On the image the Settings pages that touch the system write only under
  `/config` and `/run`**; the root is read-only there. The Time page's NTP
  server list goes to `/config/identity/chrony/sources.d/portal.sources`
  (a chrony `sourcedir` the image's `chrony.conf` names, picked up with
  `chronyc reload sources`, no restart). Disabling NTP stops chronyd, so
  with NTP off the handler writes the file and skips the reload; chronyd
  reads it at its next start, and the reply says so. An empty list removes
  the file. The NetBird page keeps `enroll.env`,
  `setup.key`, `ca.pem` and the `.enrolled` sentinel in
  `/config/identity/netbird`, the daemon's own state directory, and the image
  ships `netbird-enroll.service`; `netbird_helper.sh` generates no units. The
  daemon's identity (its WireGuard key and the management URL it logs in to)
  is `config.json` in that directory, because the image sets `NB_CONFIG`.
  A daemon that starts with that file goes back to the server in it, and
  `netbird up` then prints "Already connected" or waits on that connection
  with no timeout, so a new setup key never reaches a new server. `ca.pem`
  is one file, so saving a new server's CA replaces the old server's, and a
  daemon still holding the old identity can then no longer verify the old
  server: that is how ENG-2332 hung. Whenever the enroll unit is going to
  run, the save therefore runs `netbird down`, stops `netbird`, removes the
  identity (`clear-identity`) and starts it again; a daemon without
  `config.json` waits for a login. On an enrolled sensor the helper
  first copies `config.json`, `enroll.env`, `setup.key` and `ca.pem` into
  `previous/`. A goroutine in `handlers/netbird.go` follows the unit: on
  success it removes `previous/`, on failure it restores it, starts
  `netbird` and leaves `rollback.env`, which the status reports as a
  warning. A save, a reset and the end of a watch hold one lock
  (`netbirdChangeMu`), so their commands never run against the same files
  at once. The watch lives in the portal process, so a portal restart during
  those two minutes leaves `previous/` in place and nothing restores it. The
  status LEDs' choice is `/config/identity/status-led/conf`, written by
  `ds-status-led-watch set-unit` (see above).
  Service enable/disable and `timedatectl set-ntp` are unchanged, because
  the image is to overlay `/etc/systemd/system` from
  `/config/identity/systemd` (Plan B item B1b, not started; until then
  `systemctl enable`/`disable` from the Services and NetBird pages fail
  with EROFS on a read-only unit, and the NetBird page shows
  `enroll_on_next_boot` ticked on a fresh unit because the image preset
  enables `netbird-enroll`). Every root-side write goes through
  `ds-sensor-portal.sudoers`, which names each path exactly; a new path
  needs its line there.
- **Two of those pages are image-only now.** The Time page's NTP server list
  needs chrony with the `/config/identity/chrony/sources.d` sourcedir and
  `chronyc`, and the NetBird page needs the shipped `netbird-enroll.service`
  and `/config/identity/netbird`. A legacy deb-updated sensor
  (systemd-timesyncd, writable root) has neither, so there the NTP save
  answers 500 at `tee`/`chronyc` and a NetBird save enables a unit that does
  not exist; `postinst` says the same. The sandbox stands in for the image:
  it stubs `chronyc` and makes the sourcedir a real writable directory.
- **The version dialog (account menu) reads one request, `GET /api/v1/version`
  (`handlers/version.go`).** It joins the portal's compiled-in build, the sensor
  services' version from the last heartbeat, the image's `/etc/os-release`
  (`VERSION_ID`, `VARIANT_ID`) and the A/B update state from `ds-ota status
  --json` (meta-dsensos, ENG-1690): running and other slot, what the other
  slot holds, the last apply and its verdict, a staged package. `ds-ota` runs
  as the portal's own user with no sudo (its status reads need none: as `ds`
  with an empty environment on an A/B unit it answers in about 0.2 s). The
  `os` and `ota` sections are `null` where the source is missing, with
  `ota_error` saying why: a legacy image and the sandbox have no `ds-ota`, so
  the dialog's slot rows stay as dashes under a note naming the missing
  source. `VersionSources`
  names the file and the binary so the tests point them at a scratch file and
  a stand-in script. The portal's own schemas version is
  `go-schemas/version.Version`, a package `generate_schemas.sh` (and `build.sh`'s
  SDK mode and the Yocto recipe) writes from `git describe` of the submodule, and the sensor's is
  `HeartBeat.schemas_version`. The dialog shows both so a mismatch is visible.
- **Developer mode is shown, never changed (`handlers/devmode.go`, ENG-2159).**
  On a dev image, `ds-dev mode on` (meta-dsensos `recipes-core/ds-dev`) lets a
  developer run their own build of a ds service or of this portal from
  `/data/ds-dev` through a systemd drop-in that replaces the unit's
  `ExecStart`. The portal has no control over it; it only says so, so nobody
  takes a sideloaded unit for a shipped one. `ReadDevMode` reads three things,
  all plain file reads and never a subprocess, since it runs on every page
  render and every status poll: the mode flag
  `/config/identity/ds-dev/enabled` (present means on; `ENABLED_AT` and
  `ENABLED_BY` give since and by), the drop-ins
  `/etc/systemd/system/*.service.d/50-ds-dev.conf` (one per sideloaded unit),
  and `/data/ds-dev/<name>/ds-dev.info` beside each running copy (`NAME`,
  `UNIT`, `SOURCE_VERSION`, `SOURCE_COMMIT`, `INSTALLED_AT`). Info files from
  directories that are not a valid name (the helper's `<name>.new` and
  `<name>.old`) and with an empty `UNIT` (ds-utils, libraries only) are
  skipped. Sideloads are listed whatever the flag says, so a drop-in left
  behind with the mode off still shows. `-dev_mode_flag`, `-dev_mode_dropins`
  (a glob) and `-dev_mode_trees` move the three reads for the UI tests
  (`DevModeSources` in `Arguments.DevMode`; an empty field means the unit's
  path, so a test's `Arguments{}` reads the mode as off). It surfaces in
  three places: `templates/dev_mode_banner.html` (a warning `ds-alert`, keys
  from `devModeTemplateKeys`), included from `sidebar.html` beside the
  database banner and from `login.html`, which `GetLogin` renders with both
  banners' keys; `developer_mode` on `GET /api/v1/version` and
  `developerMode` on `GET /api/v1/status` (in the 500 body too), both
  `DevModeInfo {enabled, since, by, sideloaded}` with `sideloaded` never
  null; and the version dialog's Developer mode row. Pushing a portal build
  to a unit is `sdk-build.md`, "Putting a build on a unit".
- The sensor has one name, and it is not the portal's. `descriptive_name` is
  sensor identity: it lives in `identity.json` under `[paths] state_dir`
  (`/config/ds/state`), owned by the controller, and comes back to the portal in every heartbeat as
  `registration_entry.descriptive_name` whether or not the sensor is registered.
  Sensor HQ's record of the sensor is that field, and cot-xlate renders the same
  field into CoT messages, so setting it anywhere sets it everywhere. It is set
  through `PUT /api/v1/identity` (`handlers/identity.go`), which publishes
  `SysCom.SENSOR_IDENTITY` and waits up to a second for the controller to
  rewrite the file; the controller re-announces to Sensor HQ on its own if the
  sensor is registered. Registration (`POST /api/v1/register`) carries the
  Sensor HQ address and port. It refuses to register a sensor that has no name
  (409), because the controller will not announce one. A body may still carry
  `descriptive_name` the way clients written before the split send it (the
  fleet manager in app-mda does): the portal then sets the identity first,
  through the same code path as `PUT /api/v1/identity`, and only publishes the
  address once the controller has confirmed the name. Nothing about the name
  goes into `RegistrationCommand`; its field 1 stays reserved. A body carrying
  `owner_tak_callsign` is refused (400): no client ever sent one to the sensor.
  The TAK owner callsign is CoT configuration: `[tak] owner_callsign` in
  `config.ini`, written from the CoT connection page alongside the other TAK
  settings and read by cot-xlate at startup. Before September 2026 all three
  travelled in one `RegistrationCommand`, so a sensor could not be named without
  a Sensor HQ and renaming re-registered it; the design is in the Notion doc
  "Registration Split" (schemas #87, ds #524).
- **The side nav groups pages by the occasion they are used on, not by the
  subsystem they belong to.** Sensor overview stands alone at the top, unlabelled
  and the only entry with an icon, being where you land rather than a member of
  anything. Then Mission set up (Connections, Radio Plans, Mission profiles,
  Recording) — what you settle before a mission and change between them; Library
  (SOIs, Waveform models) — declared once and referred to by the group above;
  Captured data; and Settings, the set-once pages, in the order a sensor is
  commissioned.

  What this replaced was an unlabelled list of five shortcuts over a Detection &
  collection group, which had grown to the point of naming nothing: Connections
  and Mission profiles were shortcuts because they were used often, while Radio
  Plans and Recording were filed by subject, so two entries used in the same
  sitting sat in different halves of the column with no way to tell why. A group
  has to be nameable, and "the ones we use a lot" is not a name.

  **Settings is the one group that folds**, being nine entries deep, read at
  commissioning and left alone, and sitting under the groups worked in daily.
  Folded it gives that work the column; the others are short and are the reason
  the nav is open at all. It is an `<h3>` holding a `<button>` — the heading keeps
  the group in the page's outline for anyone skipping through landmarks, and the
  button is the control, a heading that is itself a button being neither. The
  choice is remembered in `localStorage` per browser, which is the right scope: it
  is a habit of whoever is sitting there, and the sensor has no notion of a
  person to hang it on. Storage being blocked reads as never set, so the group
  opens, which is the honest fallback rather than an error.

  It opens regardless of that when the current page is one of its own, and does
  not write that down. A group folded over the marked entry hides the one thing
  the nav has to show and makes the fold look broken; but arriving at Time is not
  a decision to keep the group open, so leaving restores the fold. Deciding this
  needs the same "is this the current page" test that lights the entry up, which
  is why both are one script — and why it runs as the parser reaches it rather
  than on `load`. Marking on `load` cost a flash of unmarked nav, which was
  tolerable; a group that folds shut after you have seen it open is not.

  Two things bite here. `.button-stack` sets `display: flex`, an author rule that
  outranks the `display: none` a browser gives `[hidden]`, so folding needs
  `.button-stack[hidden]` spelled out — the same trap as the overview's rows
  below. And the fold control is a nav item wearing a heading's type: it takes
  hover, focus and the pill radius from the `#inner-sidebar button` rules, so its
  padding has to move off the `<h3>` and onto the button, or the tint lands
  somewhere other than the thing being tinted. The 1rem left on the heading plus
  the button's 0.5rem is the 1.5rem a plain `h3` carries above its label, and the
  button's 0.5rem below is the plain one's below — so a group sits the same
  distance under its heading whichever kind it is. Read the groups off the
  rendered column rather than asserting them an entry at a time, the
  grouping being the substance of this and an ordered list being the way to see
  it go wrong.
- One page can hold more than one screen, and where it does, each screen needs an
  address. `/automations` is the mission profiles list and, with `?profile=<id>`,
  one profile's own page; `automations.ts` pushes an entry when a profile is
  opened and reads the query on load and on `popstate`. Without that, Back leaves
  the page altogether — from inside a profile it went to whatever tab was open
  before, which is not where anyone means to go. A screen worth navigating to is
  worth being able to reload, bookmark and share.

  **A profile's own page says where you are in a bar across the panel, and lists
  its cued actions in a table.** Four things moved to get there, and each has a
  reason worth keeping:

  - The trail and its back arrow sit in `.panel-topbar`, full width and under a
    rule, rather than inline above the profile's name. It belongs to the panel
    and so is padded to the panel's own edge rather than to the content column's
    gutter, which is also what tells the two apart. It is not sticky: the trail
    says which page you are on and that stays true as you scroll, while the thing
    worth keeping in reach on a long list is the heading and its buttons, which
    `.automations-section-head` already does.
  - Rename is in the profile's ⋮ menu beside Activate, and the pencil beside the
    name is gone. Renaming, duplicating, exporting and deleting a profile are
    now asked for in one place, and through the same flows the list's own cards
    use (Export opens `openProfileExportDialog` the same way a card's ⋮ does).
  - The cards became a `.ds-table` of Cue and Actions, headed by the same
    select-all that used to sit in a bar above the list. A tick belongs in the row
    it ticks, and the bar it came from existed only to hold it.
  - Import and Create are on the Cued actions heading itself, and a selection
    replaces them in place with its count, Clear and the four bulk actions. The
    import button on both this page and the list says "Import from file", the
    file being the whole of what distinguishes it from Create.

  Two traps came out of this. `display: flex` on a `<td>` replaces its
  `table-cell` and takes the cell out of its row — the browser wraps it in an
  anonymous cell, so its `border-bottom` is drawn around its own content rather
  than at the row's foot, and the rule under each row came out stepped where the
  Cue column met its neighbours. The flex goes on a box inside the cell.
  Every cell's bottom edge was measured against its row's.

  And the panel is a row elsewhere holding one column; here it stacks a bar over
  that column, so `#outer-status-div` turns into a column itself and its children
  have to stretch. That rule needs a class on it — `.panel-stacked` — and not the
  id alone: `sidebar.html` links `style.css` a second time from inside the
  `<body>`, so the shared sheet is last in the cascade on every page with a
  sidebar and beats a page-local rule of equal weight. On the id alone it lost
  `align-items` and `padding-left` to the sheet while keeping `flex-direction`,
  which the sheet does not set — a half-applied rule, which is the confusing way
  for this to fail.

  The bar is `.panel-*` in `style.css` now, three pages having one: the two
  connection pages take it to sit under Connections. Its gutter is
  `--panel-body-gutter` because the pages that took it bring their own — see
  below.
- `/connections` is the front door to the two systems a sensor talks out to, one
  card each, handing off to `/cot` and `/register`. Those were two nav entries
  filed apart, CoT above the sections and Sensor HQ down in Settings, so there
  was nowhere to see what a sensor is attached to and no reason to look in two
  places for it. One entry for both, where CoT's was.

  It has no API and no flag of its own. Each card reads the connection's own
  endpoints, and the route is gated on either flag being on (`routes.go`)
  because the page draws only the cards whose connection is enabled — the
  inverse of `/waveform_models` above, which needs both of its.

  **The two pages keep their addresses.** They are subpages by the trail across
  the top of each and by `data-also` on the nav entry, not by their paths: moving
  them under `/connections/` would break every bookmark and every link already
  pointing at them, including the overview's, to buy nothing a reader can see. So
  the sidebar's current-page logic gained a second source — an entry lights up
  for its own path, a path under it, or anything named in `data-also`. That is
  the cost of the decision, and it is one line.

  What a card may claim is the part with a decision in it, and the answer is
  less than a connections page invites you to promise. Nothing in the portal
  knows whether Sensor HQ is answering or whether a CoT message arrived: there
  is no reachability check and no delivery receipt, and the heartbeat behind the
  overview's Live/Delayed/Stale comes from a publisher on localhost — it reports
  that the sensor's own services are producing, having travelled no network
  distance, and would read "Live" with the cable pulled out. The mock for this
  page had a green "Live — just now" on the Sensor HQ card; it came out. A
  configured card says where it points and what it is set to send. The only
  thing here that finds anything out is CoT's Send test, which puts a real
  message on the wire, and it is offered only once there is somewhere to send.
  No card says live, connected, online or reachable.

  Both states of a card are in the template and `connections.ts` removes the one
  that isn't true, rather than building either: the markup is then the
  description of the page, and a state that never arrives leaves nothing behind.

  **The overview keeps a summary of it, and only of what is set up.** Its
  section is headed Connections, its rows are Sensor HQ and CoT/TAK in that
  order, and a connection that has never been set up is not a quiet row there
  but no row at all — if neither is set up the section is not on the page. The
  overview reports a sensor's working state; an empty row saying a thing could
  exist is a job for the page that lists every connection there is, which now
  exists and is one click up the nav. The cost is that the card and both rows are
  drawn hidden and shown when the fetches answer, since only the client knows
  which. A section arriving a moment late beats a heading standing over nothing
  while the page finds out whether it has anything to say.

  **Position sources is the one card on the overview that changes a setting,
  and it does not believe its own write.** Each receiver row has a switch that
  writes `[orientation] disabled_sources` through `POST /api/v1/config`, the
  same file the Configuration page edits. Processing reads that file once, at
  startup, and reports what it is running with as `enabled` on each row of
  `procStatus.orientationSources`. So the card has two truths to show and
  shows both: the switch is drawn from the file, read back through `GET
  /api/v1/config` after every write and on every refresh, never from the
  click; the row's state ("In use", "Off") is the heartbeat's; and a row where
  the two disagree says "Off after processing restarts" beside the state, with
  the shared processing restart notice above the rows offering the restart.
  The notice follows the data, not the click: it is up whenever any row
  disagrees, after a reload or a change made from the Configuration page too,
  and comes down when the heartbeat catches up. A
  switch that flipped on the click would say the receiver was off while the
  file still said on; a row that flipped with the switch would say it while
  processing was still using it. Until the file has been read at all the
  switches are disabled, since a switch drawn from a guess is a lie either way.

  That is what made `.ds-card-rows-divided` ask about visible rows rather than
  about children — `*:not([hidden]) ~ *:not([hidden])` for the rule, `:has()` for
  the last row. Position selectors counted the hidden one: dropping the *first*
  of two rows left a rule over the top of the survivor and a top padding it no
  longer wanted. With nothing hidden the new rules pick out exactly the same
  rows, so every other divided card is unchanged. The one-of-two case is the one
  worth checking for that reason, dropping Sensor HQ rather than CoT because
  dropping the second row would have passed either way.

  `/cot` and `/cot/connection` are otherwise untouched, legacy tabs and all,
  which is why `--panel-body-gutter` exists: their wrapper centres itself inside
  a max-width with its own padding, and the shared body's gutter would have
  stacked on top of it. `/register` had its shell converted first — a heading
  on the scale, the shared column, tokens for spacing — with its `.container`
  boxes and original form left alone, and then the forms in September 2026:
  the boxes are `ds-card`s, the fields `ds-text-input` with the component's
  helper and inline message, the buttons `ds-button`, and the forms reuse the
  task pages' `.task-form` layout classes. Nothing on the page is sized or
  coloured by the page.
- **`/register` is two steps in the order they have to happen.** The name step
  (a `PUT /api/v1/identity` form) is always on the page; the registration step
  (the current registration and the `POST /api/v1/register` form) is hidden
  until the sensor has a name, and `register.ts` opens it as soon as a save is
  confirmed rather than waiting for the next identity poll. Sensor HQ needs the
  name and the server refuses a nameless registration, so showing the second
  form to a nameless sensor only ever produced a disabled button and a link to
  the Sensor overview. Both pages set the same name (see the identity note
  above); the register page has its own form so the flow finishes where it
  started.
- `/soi` is the SOIs and `/waveform_models` the models that used to be half of
  `/model`, one page each in the nav above Mission profiles. They were two tabs
  of one Detection library page at `/soi`, with the tab named in the URL hash
  (`/soi#detectors`); a tab is only findable once the page holding it has been
  found, and nothing about an SOI says to look behind a second tab for the models
  its logic is written in terms of. Two addresses also means the browser's back
  button is the whole of the navigation between them.

  The models' API is still `/api/v1/model/xgboost/*` behind the model feature
  flag, and the page reads `/api/v1/soi` to name what a deletion would stop
  matching, so `/waveform_models` needs both flags — the gate the tab had, now
  stated on the route (`handlers/automation.go`, beside `/soi`'s) and on the nav
  entry. A page can depend on two features.

  What the two pages share is their shape: a sticky bar with a search field over
  a table, under `.library-*` in `style.css` rather than in either page's
  `<style>`. The name is the Detection library's, kept because it names a shape
  both pages still have. The two bars were measured against each other rather
  than against numbers, which is what stops them drifting.

  `/model` keeps the TensorRT engine and is now Embedding models, under Settings.
  The SOIs table gives waveform models a column of their own, apart from the
  parameters: `soiFacts` in `widgets/automation_common.ts` splits them, asking
  `/api/v1/soi/logic_variables` which probabilities come from an imported model
  rather than from the signal record's own `*_prob` fields. Only a model's chip
  carries its threshold. The engine's built-in modulation probabilities stay
  where they are, as parameters — that column is imported models and nothing
  else, and a proposal to fold the modulations in beside them was considered and
  dropped.

  **A model the sensor has not got still belongs in that column**, dimmed, with
  Tabler's alert-circle before its name and "Model missing" on the pointer. The
  split had been "is this variable a classifier the sensor publishes?", which put
  an imported SOI's unknown model under parameters — the one column that is
  supposed to be what the sensor *measures*. `isMissingModel` is the third
  answer: a probability the sensor publishes nowhere and that is not one of
  `MODULATIONS`. It is false until `/api/v1/soi/logic_variables` has resolved,
  since before that every modulation looks equally unknown. The mark goes on the
  `.dsds-chip` the component paints rather than the `ds-chip` host, which is
  `display: contents` and so has no box to dim or to hover.

  **The identity is a symbol against the name, not a column.** It had a column of
  its own, headed Affiliation, carrying the SIDC frame and the word — and the
  word was costing 130px of a table that could not go below 958px. The frame is
  the standard's way of saying this and the word only repeats it, so the word
  went to the cell's title, beside the name that was already truncating there.
  The import review's cards had drawn it this way all along, which is the reason
  to prefer it over shortening the header: one shape for an SOI wherever one
  appears. Search still matches the word, and matters more for doing so now that
  it is not on the page — a symbol is not something anyone can type.

  **A table too wide for its column pins its actions rather than losing them.**
  `.ds-table-wrap` scrolls, and on macOS that scrollbar is invisible until you
  are already scrolling, so an overflowing table looks like one that fits with
  its last column clipped against the edge of the card. What goes off the side is
  the row's actions, they being at the far end and the one thing every row is
  there to offer.

  So `widgets/table_scroll.ts` measures the wrap and sets two classes, and
  `.ds-table-actions` pins to the right edge while there is anything underneath
  it. Both facts are needed and they are not the same fact: whether the table
  overflows decides the pin, and whether it is scrolled short of the end decides
  the cue. It watches rather than measures once, the overflow being a function of
  the window — the page column is capped and the sidebar fixed — and of rows that
  arrive after it runs and change width when a search filters them.

  Three details are each a bug avoided. The pinned cell needs an opaque fill or
  the columns sliding beneath show through, and a selected row's tint is 12% blue,
  so the tint goes over that fill as a gradient rather than instead of it —
  `background-color` is one layer and that layer has to be the one you cannot see
  through. `thead` paints across the whole table rather than per cell, so a pinned
  header cell slides out from under its own background unless it carries a copy.

  Pinning the header cell is also what turned up a fill nobody could see. The
  legacy `tr:nth-child(odd)` rule near the top of `style.css` paints the first
  row of every table, and a `thead`'s row is the first row — so the header band
  had always been `--dsds-bg-recessed`, and the `--dsds-bg-moderate` that
  `.ds-table thead` asks for two lines above it was dead code. `tbody tr` resets
  the stripe; the head never did. The pinned cell painted the token the rule
  actually names and so came out three values lighter than the row it sat in,
  which is how a difference too small to notice on its own became visible.
  `.ds-table thead tr` now clears the stripe, so the band is the colour the rule
  intends. It was sampled either side of the pin as pixels, computed style
  having agreed the whole time that they matched.

  **The cue is two marks, each saying half of it**: a 1px rule in
  `--dsds-border-base` at the column's leading edge, and a `--dsds-shadow-04`
  gradient over 6px beside it. The rule is where the column begins; the gradient
  is that something continues under it. `--dsds-border-base` is the token the row
  rules already use, so the line is one the table draws elsewhere rather than a
  new kind of mark, and `--dsds-shadow-04` is the floor of the shadow scale —
  there is nothing fainter to reach for.

  Which is why the last round of softening had to come out of the spread. Spread
  is what reads as height: the same 4% over 12px is a column lifted off the page,
  and over 6px a shelf sitting just above it. Same ink, less elevation.

  It took five attempts and every one was too strong, which is the useful part.
  A `box-shadow` on the cell was invisible at three times zoom, cells in a
  `border-collapse` table being poor shadow casters. A gradient at 14% over 12px
  read as grubby, and at 8% over 8px still read as a shade. A 1px rule in
  `--dsds-border-strong` was crisp and precise, and read as a column divider —
  a thing this table has none of, so it announced a boundary where the message is
  "there is more underneath". 4% over 12px was faint and still floated. Alone,
  either mark says the wrong thing: the rule is a border, the gradient is a smudge
  with no edge to belong to. The instinct each round was to make it clearer, and
  clearer is what got it wrong four times.

  Both are pseudo-elements rather than a `border-left` or a `box-shadow`, because
  a cell border under `border-collapse` is shared with the neighbour and resolved
  against it — asking for one moves the column rather than marking it — and this
  way the width and the falloff are the stylesheet's to state.

  Whichever it is, it matters more than it sounds, because pinning *removed* the
  only hint there had been. Before, a column clipped at the card's edge said
  something was missing; after, the last column looks deliberate and nothing
  suggests it is standing on top of anything.

  **Pinning the leading columns instead is the commoner convention, and was built
  and looked at before this was settled.** Freeze panes, Airtable's primary field,
  the pinned-left default in most grid libraries: it is what a reader expects, and
  it costs nothing at rest, the tick and the name already being where they would
  have been.

  Pinning the actions does have a cost, and it is worth knowing rather than
  arguing away. How much of each column is both on screen and not painted over
  was measured directly, and what the pin takes is the count column —
  buried behind it rather than clipped at the card's edge, which is the worse of
  the two, a hidden column leaving no account of where it went. How much depends
  on how far the table overflows, which depends on the window and on how long the
  names in it happen to be: 92 of its 118px survived in one measured window and 9
  in another, so the worst case is the whole column. Measure it in the window at
  hand rather than quoting a figure.

  The trade was made anyway: the actions are what every row is there to offer,
  having them under the pointer without scrolling first is the thing this whole
  section exists for, and the count is a single figure and the least read of the
  six. Taking the Affiliation column out is what makes it affordable, by leaving
  so little to scroll that the name never fully leaves either.

  **The floor is what to argue with, and it is mostly headers and chips.**
  The per-column floors and the window width where each table gives were
  measured, and squeezing the whole table is the only honest
  way to ask — `min-content` on one cell of an auto-layout table changes nothing,
  the algorithm redistributing around it. Taking out the Affiliation column was
  the first pass at this.

  **The count column's heading was the second, and it wraps rather than clips.**
  It is the one column whose floor is entirely its own heading: the body holds a
  single digit, so nothing underneath is holding it open. Lifting `nowrap` for
  that one cell drops its minimum to the longer of its two words, 82px against
  122, while its maximum stays the whole phrase — so the table algorithm gives it
  one line where there is room and folds it to two where there is not. The table's
  floor went 744px to 708px and it now fits a 1024px window it used to scroll at,
  first giving at 960px.

  Which is 36px, and worth saying plainly because the heading looked like it was
  worth eighty. It is not: the column cannot go below its longest word, and the
  only way to the low forties is clipping. The comparison was made both ways —
  clipping reaches 48px and renders the heading as "C…", which
  is not a shortened label but the absence of one, and the word it drops first is
  the half carrying the meaning. Wrapping buys about half as much and hides
  nothing, and there is no breakpoint in it to keep in step with a floor that
  moves with the content. Before it, a cap on the cell was established as the
  only thing that moves a nowrap cell's floor at all: neither an inner box with
  `overflow: hidden` nor a flex item at `min-width: 0` shifted it off 118px, a
  cell's minimum being its content's minimum whatever is painted over the top.

  What is left, in order of what it would buy: the name's 14rem cap and the two
  chip columns at ~168px each.

  The waveform models table never scrolls at any width — `table-layout: fixed` at
  thirds — so it crowds rather than overflowing, and none of the above applies to
  it. That is a separate question and deliberately still open.

  A profile's cued actions are the third `.ds-table`, and it calls
  `watchTableScrolling` too — it holds chips of unbounded length in both of its
  columns, so it can overflow, and the pin and the cue are the same answer here as
  there. Nothing about the mechanism is SOI-specific; anything added to it should
  be checked on this table as well.

  **The two chip cells carry class names so nothing has to count columns.**
  `soi-cell-parameters` and `soi-cell-model`. Three checks addressed them as
  `td:nth-child(4)` and `(5)` and all three broke the moment Affiliation went;
  the column order is not a contract and should not be treated as one.

  **An SOI has two editors, and which one opens is decided by the logic.**
  `widgets/soi_form.ts` is the Create new SOI form — a 600px dialog
  (`<ds-dialog size="md">`, whose token *is* 600px) asking for the five facts an
  SOI is usually written from, in the units a person says them in.
  `widgets/logic_builder.ts` is the older editor and still writes any logic the
  interpreter evaluates. The form is a second door into the same room, not a
  layer over it: it emits exactly the shapes the builder parses, so nothing
  round-trips wrongly between them.

  | Parameter | Variable | Form unit | Logic unit | Shape |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | Frequency range | `sig_center_freq` | MHz | Hz | window or one-sided bound |
  | Bandwidth | `sig_bandwidth` | MHz | Hz | window or one-sided bound |
  | Modulation type | seven signal `*_prob` | — | 0–1 | `{">": [{"var"}, 0.5]}`, OR-ed |
  | Pulse duration | `pulse_duration_s` | seconds | seconds | window or one-sided bound |
  | Pulse repetition interval | `pulse_rep_interval_s` | seconds | seconds | window or one-sided bound |
  | Waveform model | a classifier variable | % | 0–1 | `{">": [{"var"}, pct/100]}` |

  **Emitter class is not a field on `SoiDefinition`.** The form still requires
  one (Radar / Comms / Electronic attack / Other) and the table shows it as a
  subtle chip (parameters and models are outlined), so `compile` writes an always-true comparison
  `{"==": ["__soi_emitter_class__:comms", "__soi_emitter_class__:comms"]}` into
  the rule. The interpreter accepts it (`verifyJsonLogic` allows `==` of two
  strings) and the match is unchanged. `readSoi` skips that node so the form
  can reopen; `inferEmitterClass` reads it for the table; `formatLogic` omits
  it from tooltips. Radar can still be reinvented without the mark — Pulse
  duration, PRI, or `radar` locked at the sensor's gate score. Do not add a sibling key on
  the root `{and: …}` object: the validator demands exactly one operator.

  **The Radar lock score is ds's, not the portal's.** Processing extracts pulse
  duration and PRI only for a signal whose built-in `radar` score clears
  `[pdw_stats] ml_radar_gate_threshold` in config.ini (PdwStatsExtractor). The
  portal reads the same key from the merged config.ini and serves it as
  `gate_threshold` on the built-in radar variable (`withRadarGate`,
  handlers/logic_variables.go); Radar mode locks the rule to it
  (`radarThresholdPercent`). The two agree because they read one file. A
  variable list without the field is an older sensor, and the lock falls back to
  50, which is ds's own default for the key. The old 90 survives only in
  `hasRadarModelLock`, to read SOIs written then. Never give the portal a radar
  score of its own again.

  All five have a backing variable; that was checked against
  `/api/v1/soi/logic_variables` rather than assumed. The MHz-to-Hz conversion
  snaps to milliHz exactly as `toBaseUnits` in the builder does, so a frequency
  typed here and re-read there is the same number.

  **All four measured parameters are Min/Max pairs, and either box may be left
  empty.** Both filled is a window, `{"<=": [lo, {"var"}, hi]}`; a minimum alone
  is `{">=": [{"var"}, lo]}` and a maximum alone `{"<=": [{"var"}, hi]}`. Pulse
  duration and PRI were briefly a single field with a `≤` in front of it, which
  the designer replaced with the pair for consistency with the two above them.
  The one-sided bound is what keeps that from costing anything: "pulses of at
  least 2 ms, however long they run" stays sayable, and nobody has to invent an
  upper limit that then quietly excludes a signal. All three shapes are ones the
  builder draws, so a bound written here reopens here as the same one field.

  **A row's type picker lists the types no other row is using, and two
  exceptions.** A single-use type another row holds is *absent* from this row's
  list. It used to be listed and greyed, with "already added" in the row's note,
  and the designer replaced that: a row you cannot pick is a row somebody reads
  before finding out it was never on offer. The two exceptions are what keep the
  list from being purely "unused types", and both are load-bearing rather than
  concessions. A row always lists **its own current type**, or the field would
  have nothing to show as selected. And **Frequency range** is always listed,
  being the one type that may repeat — which is also what makes Add parameter an
  action that can never fail, since the row it adds is always a frequency one.

  It follows that no two rows' lists are necessarily the same length: with all
  five types in use the frequency row lists only itself and every other row lists
  itself and Frequency range. **That is the accepted cost of the pattern and not
  a bug to normalise** — the designer chose it over two alternatives that were
  drawn beside it, and a check expecting one length would be a check asking for
  the pattern to be undone. `typesFor` in `soi_form.ts` is the one statement of
  the rule, and both exceptions, the absence, and the differing lengths were
  checked against it.

  **Every row can be removed, including the last, and the section has somewhere
  for that to land.** Nothing ever forbade it — an SOI may be a waveform model
  and nothing else, and `compile` refuses a draft with neither rather than
  refusing the removal that got there. What was missing was the state: a recessed
  block (`--dsds-bg-recessed`, `--dsds-radius-base`) saying "Add a parameter to
  describe the signal this SOI should match.", with **Add parameter moved inside
  it** so there is one thing to do rather than a sentence and a button that
  belong together with nothing saying so. The button is moved rather than
  duplicated — one element, one id, one listener — which works because
  `defineComponents` builds a custom element once and ignores the reconnection a
  move causes. The rows box and the line the button sits on are hidden rather
  than left empty, an empty flex child still costing the section its 16px gap.

  Add parameter is a `filled-neutral` `ds-button` at `sm`, which *is* the
  design's 32px box, 12px inset and `--dsds-neutral-bg-muted` fill; nothing about
  it is written out in `soi.html` any more. Two places where the three mock
  artboards for this pattern disagree with the modal as implemented, both
  resolved in the implementation's favour and neither silently: the mocks draw
  the Add parameter label at 14px where a `sm` button's own scale is 12px (see
  `design-system-components.md`), and their Parameters card reproduces the
  *stale* leftmost modal variant's 14px/500 heading and 12px section gap rather
  than the `text-base-semi` heading and 16px gap the two agreeing variants have.
  The mocks also draw a trailing check on the selected row, which the design
  system's own MenuItem renders and `ds-menu-button` has never carried for any
  caller — left alone, since adding it would change the affiliation picker in the
  same breath and nobody asked for that.

  **The modulation row is a multi-select, and it is the one place the form emits
  an OR.** A signal is one modulation, but an SOI is usually written for a family
  of emitters that are not all the same one, so several ticks mean any of them —
  one tick compiles to the bare condition and several to `{"or": [ … ]}`, which
  the builder draws as an ANY group. The row stays single-use: one row, many
  ticks. Two different numbers are in play and **they must not be unified**:
  modulations compile at 0.5, waveform models default to 0.65. A modulation is
  one of seven guesses the sensor makes about every signal and half the vote is
  where it becomes the sensor's answer — the same 50% App MDA classifies at, and
  what the note in the menu tells the reader. A model is a yes-or-no question
  somebody trained on purpose.

  The seven modulations, and their App MDA names, are `MODULATIONS` in
  `widgets/automation_common.ts` — ASK, AM, PSK, FSK, FM, QAM, CHIRP against
  `ask_prob`, `analog_am_prob`, `psk_prob`, `fsk_prob`, `analog_fm_prob`,
  `constellation_prob`, `chirp_prob`. `variableLabel` consults that table first,
  so the chips on this page say what the picker that wrote them said; without it
  QAM would come back as "Constellation" and AM as "Analog AM". **The sensor
  publishes fourteen `*_prob` variables and this list is seven of them** — analog
  PM, OOK, CSS, FHSS, hopping, channelized and OFDM have no App MDA name, so
  they are not offered and an SOI testing one can only be edited in the builder,
  never created. That is the cost of one shared vocabulary, taken deliberately;
  adding a label to the table is all it takes to offer one again.

  `readSoi` is strict: every condition has to be recognised and nothing may move.
  An OR holding anything but modulations fails it — that is a rule about
  alternatives, and this form would show it as a set of ticks and save it as
  something else. So does a NOT, nesting, a second model, a variable with no
  row — and, under Radar, a model other than `pulse_radar`, since the lock would
  show `pulse_radar` in its place and save it there. Opening a Radar SOI applies
  the lock but keeps a stored `pulse_radar` confidence rather than moving it to
  90; only a rule with no model gains the package.

  **Two shapes it used to refuse and now reads, because refusing them cost more
  than reading them.** Measured by opening every SOI on a sensor and counting how
  many the form can state: it was **one of six**, and the one was a bare frequency
  window. Every SOI anybody had actually written was refused.

  A modulation at a confidence other than 0.5 was the refusal that hit all five,
  because real SOIs are written at 0.7 and 0.8 while a tick means 0.5. The tick
  stays numberless — that was the designer's call and it was not revisited — so
  the number is *remembered instead of shown*: `readSoi` files it in
  `modulationThresholds` and `compile` writes it back. A modulation ticked in the
  dialog has no entry and gets the default. The cost is a value on the draft that
  nothing on screen accounts for, so two SOIs that look identical in the form can
  compile differently. That is worth less than loosening a rule from 0.8 to 0.5
  every time somebody corrects a name, which is what the alternative did.

  `<` and `>` on a range are read as `<=` and `>=`. Three of the five were
  written "bandwidth under 100 kHz", and the form has one Max field that writes
  `<=`, so the round trip moves a signal sitting exactly on the boundary from
  excluded to included. One hertz at the edge of a band chosen in round numbers,
  against a form that could not open the SOI at all.

  It used to decide **which editor opened**, with the builder taking anything it
  refused and "Edit logic" in a row's ⋮ reaching the builder on demand. Neither
  is true now: a row has one edit control, the pencil, and it opens the form for
  every SOI. `readSoi` still runs on open, and what it decides is narrower —
  whether the dialog is showing the SOI's rule or only its name, and so whether
  to raise the warning at the top of the body. **The form opens on an SOI it
  cannot state with the rule sections hidden, and saving writes the stored rule
  back untouched** — the name, identity and emitter class are the form's to
  change, the rule is not. It used to open with the rule blank and save the
  blank one, a rewrite the designer accepted on the condition it was never
  silent; the notice was that condition. Review of PR #168 found that a warning
  alone still let a name correction flatten a rule, so the rewrite now takes a
  click of its own: **Replace rule**, in the notice, brings the sections up
  blank and Save compiles them from there. `heldLogic` in
  `widgets/soi_form.ts` is the state behind this.

  So strictness now buys honesty rather than routing, and it is worth no less: a
  lax `readSoi` would not open a different editor, it would drop a condition on
  save without saying anything.

  **The builder can still write these rules and is the only thing that can, but
  nothing on the page offers it.** It opens only as the fallback for the form
  having failed to wire — which is also the only way to reach "Edit JSON
  directly", so that went with "Edit logic". An SOI whose rule the form cannot
  state therefore arrives by import or by a detection bundle, and cannot be
  edited in place — only kept as it is or replaced whole. That is the price of
  one edit dialog, paid knowingly.

  A bare condition at the root comes back wrapped in an AND on save. That is not
  the form taking a liberty: `parseGroup` in the builder normalises it the same
  way, so an AND of one is what both editors write.

  `AFFILIATION_CHOICES` in `widgets/automation_common.ts` collapses the identity
  list to one row per symbol: joker and faker are the exercise forms of suspect
  and hostile and the standard draws each pair in one frame, so the picker offers
  `Suspect / Joker` and `Hostile / Faker`. **Choosing one stores the plain
  identity.** A plainly-labelled row must not quietly mark data as an exercise,
  and nothing in the portal sets up an exercise to belong to. But `covers` is
  what a row answers to, so an SOI already holding `JOKER` shows that row
  selected and keeps `JOKER` through a save that did not touch the field —
  opening a form is not editing it.

  **The order those rows are shown in is the picker's, not
  `AFFILIATION_CHOICES`'.** `AFFILIATION_FAMILIES` in `soi_form.ts` groups them
  by frame — quatrefoil, rectangle, square, diamond, then the two that decline to
  say anything — with the confirmed identity first in each family and its dashed,
  unconfirmed form directly under it. A dashed outline only means "the
  unconfirmed version of the thing above"; six rows away from the thing it
  qualifies it means nothing, which is what `AFFILIATIONS`' order (the common
  four, then the rest) produced. The families are divided by the design system's
  own `MenuSection` — see `design-system-components.md`.

  `AFFILIATIONS` itself is deliberately left in its original order, because two
  other things read it as an order: the logic builder's SOI dialog builds its
  native `<select>` of all eleven identities straight from it (`soi.ts`), and
  `AFFILIATION_CHOICES[0]` — Unknown — is the identity a new SOI starts on.
  Anything `AFFILIATION_FAMILIES` fails to name still gets a row, in a family of
  its own at the end: the standard is not going to grow an identity, but this is
  the portal's only way to set one and a picker that silently drops a row is
  worse than a picker with an oddly placed one.

  **The form's section headings are the Text ramp, not the Heading ramp.**
  Parameters and Waveform model are `dsds-type-text-size-base dsds-type-semibold`
  — the design's `--dsds-text-base-semi-*` — and the line under Waveform model is
  `dsds-type-text-size-sm`. `dsds-type-heading-md` is the same 16px/600/24px but
  tightens tracking, which the design does not ask for, so the two are not
  interchangeable here. The resolved pixels were checked rather than the class
  names, because what was wrong before was the token and not the markup.
  `.soi-form-heading-group` puts no gap between a heading and its line,
  which is the agreeing variants' value: 24px of leading over 20px is already a
  block, and anything added to it starts the second line reading as its own row.

  A caution for anyone re-reading the designer's Paper file: the modal is drawn
  there three times side by side, and **the leftmost variant is stale.** It is
  the one that still carries the pre-change header values (raw 14px/`sm-medium`
  headings, the description at `xs`), a 12px section gap, and a 2px
  heading-to-description gap; the middle and right variants agree with each other
  on `text-base-semi` headings, `text-sm-normal` descriptions, a 16px section gap
  and no heading-to-description gap at all, which is what the implementation
  matches. Read the right-hand pair. The compact dropzone, the "or" beside it and
  the new model description copy are drawn in only one variant each, so those have
  no second opinion to check against — and for the dropzone that one drawing is in
  the *stale* variant, it being the only one that draws the box at all. Its
  geometry is still the only statement of that box the file contains, and
  `design-system-components.md` records what it says, which is a centred 70px row
  rather than the stacked 96px column it is easy to assume.

  To the right of those three sit three more artboards, the patterns for the
  Parameters section that the type-picker note above came out of. They are their
  own drawing rather than a fourth modal variant, and where they restate
  something the modal already settled they restate the stale version of it — so
  read them for the pattern and not for the frame around it.

  The form was driven end to end, including what actually reaches the sensor and
  reading it straight back into the form to prove the round trip. The SOIs the run
  needed — a joker, one whose OR the form must refuse — were written for it
  rather than taken from fixtures that have neither, and any left behind by a run
  that fails before its own cleanup have to be deleted: two SOIs of one name
  leave the lookup picking the older one, which is a confusing way to fail. The
  form's rendered spacing and control metrics were measured too, which is how to
  answer a question about the layout without reading numbers off a screenshot.

  Reaching the builder at all takes work, now that nothing on the page offers it.
  The form is suppressed by patching `document.getElementById` to return null for
  `soi-form-dialog`, which is what makes `initSoiForm` return null and the pencil
  fall back to the builder — the real fallback path, driven deliberately. Patch
  the lookup rather than removing the markup, because the page reads it on load
  and a reload would put it back. An SOI written for the run has to be edited,
  creating one there being no longer possible. A scratch SOI carrying
  `{">": [{"var": "snr"}, 10]}` is the deliberate case: the builder keeps the
  comparison when the variable changes, and only reaches for its 0.65 default
  when the value field is *blank*, so the field has to be emptied before
  switching to a detector. Ten dB of SNR is not ten per cent confidence,
  and it is right that the builder keeps a number somebody typed.

  **The SOI import decides everything in the browser and sends one request.**
  `widgets/soi_import.ts` reads the file, says what each SOI in it would do to
  this sensor, and posts the agreed ones to `/api/v1/sois/import` once, with
  `on_conflict=overwrite` as the policy for the lot. That is not a trick played on
  the handler. `ImportSois` in `handlers/automation.go` asks one question per
  record — do I already have this id? — so a record carrying an unknown id is
  added, one carrying a known id is EDITed in place, and one carrying no id at all
  gets a fresh uuid and is added. Per-row behaviour therefore falls out of *how
  each record is prepared*, and the client is the only thing that has to know the
  difference. The EDIT is what matters most: an overwrite keeps the uuid, so every
  automation pointing at that SOI goes on resolving, which a delete-and-reinsert
  would break silently.

  The uuid is identity, and the six cases follow from that. A new id imports as it
  stands (**a**); a matching id with the same name and logic is already here and is
  shown nowhere at all (**b**); a matching id with either differing overwrites,
  keeping the uuid (**c**); logic naming a waveform model this sensor has not got
  imports and sits inert until the model lands (**d**); and a *different* id under
  a name this sensor already uses imports as "Name (2)", whether the logic differs
  (**e**) or matches exactly (**f**). e and f are the pair worth understanding:
  the same rule under a different uuid is evidence somebody copied an SOI rather
  than moved it, and that is worth showing rather than resolving. Neither is
  refused and neither overwrites — two SOIs of one name is a list nobody can read,
  so the arriving one is numbered exactly as an arriving mission profile is, and
  the sensor's own keeps the name people know it by. c is the one destructive case
  and the one that carries an id up; e and f are sent with an **empty** id, which
  is what stops the server treating them as a new version of the SOI holding the
  name. Duplicates within one file are folded together before the review is drawn,
  on the uuid where there is one.

  **Every flagged row starts unticked, not only the overwrite.** The written spec
  had c unticked and d, e and f ticked with their notes; the designer's frame
  draws every flagged card with an empty box and counts only the rest beside
  Import, and the frame is what is implemented. It is also the more defensible of
  the two: a row was flagged because it was worth stopping on, and a row worth
  stopping on is a row somebody should have to agree to. `flagged` is the one
  statement of which rows those are — everything except case a, so the overwrite,
  the two that land renamed, and anything naming a waveform model this sensor has
  not got, which is exactly the rows that carry a note. `row.include =
  !flagged(row)` is the one statement of the default and Select all reads the same
  predicate, so the three cannot drift apart.

  **The Min signal strength (RSSI) field takes −200 to −1 dB, and a value
  outside that holds the save.** It opens at −200, the floor being the setting
  that turns nobody away, so a field revealed and left alone matches everything
  the automation would have matched without it.

  It is saved as a condition all the same, which is the part that changed. The
  field used to open at 0 dB and 0 was dropped on save: a value nobody would
  ever set could be read as "never mind", so a field opened and closed again
  cost nothing. With a real range 0 is out of it, and a number that both means
  something and means nothing is not worth keeping — the way to say there is no
  threshold is to remove the field, which is what the button beside it has
  always been for. So a card can now read "Min RSSI −200 dB" where it used to
  read "Always reacts", and the two mean the same thing.

  An empty field is not zero: taking `Number('')` off the input gives 0, which is
  out of range, so a field with nothing in it yet would be told off about a
  number nobody typed. It reads as `NaN` and fails the range check on its own
  terms. Cover both ends of the range and both ways out of it, a bound written
  the wrong way round being the sort that passes at one
  end.

  The bounds go on the input as `min`/`max` as well as into the check, so the
  field is out of range to anything reading the page rather than only to us. The
  message is a `role="alert"` paragraph that is also the input's
  `aria-describedby` — the role reads it out when it appears, the description is
  what makes it readable again on the way back to the field.

  **Signal power is stripped from the logic rather than being a reason to refuse
  it, and the server still refuses it.** `validateSoiDefinition` rejects any
  detection logic naming `sig_power_dbm` or `sig_power_dbfs`, and that is correct
  and is staying: signal strength is an automation Condition now, not something an
  SOI evaluates. But an SOI written before that move is still an SOI and the rest
  of its rule is still worth having, so `stripSignalPower` takes the power test out
  on the way up and the server never sees one. No backend change, and the server
  keeps being the thing that would catch a client that stopped doing this.

  It is done **silently** — no note, no alert, no flag — on the designer's
  judgement that it is rare enough not to warrant feedback; a stripped record lands
  in the list unflagged and ticked like any other. Where the test sits makes no difference:
  inside an `and`, inside an `or`, or under a `not`, all three are treated alike,
  which is a decision rather than an oversight, since dropping a branch of an `or`
  or the operand of a `not` can change what a rule *means* rather than merely
  relaxing it. Two shapes have to be tidied on the way back up or the result is
  logic no editor here would draw: an `and` or `or` left with one operand collapses
  to that operand rather than staying a group of one — which is what `parseGroup`
  in the builder would do to it anyway — and a `not` whose operand has gone goes
  with it. Everything not being removed is passed through by reference and a rule
  with no power test in it is returned as the very string that arrived, never
  re-serialised, so an import that changes nothing changes nothing. If nothing
  survives, the SOI cannot be imported, the sensor refusing empty logic.

  Three things stop a record outright — no name, unreadable, and nothing left after
  the strip — and all three are said **once, in a warning beside the file**, rather
  than as a section of rows that cannot be ticked. A row in a list of things to
  tick that cannot be ticked is a row somebody reads twice, and every one of these
  fails for a reason about the file rather than a decision anybody is being asked
  to make. When *every* record fails, the same warning says so as a fact about the
  file ("No SOIs in this file can be imported") instead of leaving an empty review
  behind it. Unrecognized fields are dropped silently and unknown enum
  values become `UNRECOGNIZED`: that is the generated parser's own behaviour, it is
  wanted, and nothing here should grow a check that breaks it. It is also why
  `analyse` is handed the raw `JSON.parse` array beside the decoded records —
  something that was never an SOI decodes into a nameless one rather than failing,
  so what cannot be read has to be spotted before the decoder tidies it away.

  **The review is one flat list, flagged rows first.** It was two collapsible
  sections — what needed deciding, open, over what did not, folded away — and the
  designer replaced them after using the built version: both headings, their
  counts and their chevrons are gone, and what is left is a "Review for import"
  heading, a rule, the Select all row hanging under it, and one list. The flagged
  rows come first and unticked, and a neutral Chip at the far end of that row says
  "N possible issues", so a flag ten rows down is mentioned somewhere other than
  on its own row. Nothing was gained by asking a reader to open a section before
  they could see what was in it.

  Within each group the list keeps the order the file gave them, sorted on nothing
  else: a file's order is the only order it states, and an alphabetical list of
  SOIs would stop matching the file it came from. The testid on a card is its
  place in the drawn list, so it names the row a reader is looking at rather than
  a row of the file.

  **Select all stands for every card in the list**, and is the plain tri-state:
  ticked when they are all on, dashed when some are, empty when none is, and a
  press takes the whole list or clears it. Which press this is comes off the rows
  and not off the input's own `checked`, the box being a picture of the ticks
  below it rather than a state of its own.

  It takes the flagged rows too. The care about overwrites lives in what a file
  *arrives* at — a flagged row starts unticked and stays that way until somebody
  acts — and not in holding rows back from a box labelled "all", which is a
  promise the box then quietly breaks. Pressing it turns the flagged rows visibly
  on, which is the person doing it.

  Two earlier attempts are worth knowing about, because both are reachable again
  by reasoning from "it must never agree to an overwrite" alone. Ticking only the
  unflagged rows makes the first press dead, since a file arrives with exactly
  those already on; making the box show only the rows it would tick fixes the dead
  press but then draws a full box beside `(4 of 8 selected)` and leaves "select
  all" meaning "select some". The display and what a press does have to answer to
  the same set, and that set is the whole list.

  Every number in the dialog reads the same two figures — what is ticked, and how
  many rows there are — from one function, so `(N of M selected)` beside Select all
  and `Import (N)` in the footer cannot disagree. The frame drew three numbers that
  did, and none of them is copied.

  The layout was page-local to `soi.html` because it *was* different from its two
  sibling imports — they had a heading above their list where this has a heading, a
  rule and a select-all row, they coloured this count as a line of warning text
  where this is a neutral chip, and their alert spanned the card where this one sits
  in the row's own column. Only the genuinely shared parts went to
  `widgets/import_review.ts`.

  Both of those two have since followed, the mission profiles dialog first and the
  cued actions dialog after it: each now has the rule under a "Review for import"
  heading, a labelled chosen file, the neutral chip counting messages, notes rather
  than warnings, every card ticked, flagged cards first, and this page's spacing to
  the pixel. They are still drawn by `automations.ts` rather than sharing code with
  this — the three lists hold different things, a profile having cued actions nested
  inside it — so what is shared is the frame and the wording rather than the
  implementation, and what holds them together is that all three were measured in
  the browser against each other. The one difference left between the two automations
  dialogs is where a card's alerts sit, and it is deliberate; see
  `.ai/design-system-components.md`. Anything moved into `widgets/import_review.ts`
  from here should be checked against all three, not just against this page.

  Two things in here look like slack and are the design's, so they should not be
  tidied away. The row naming the chosen file is the design system's *field* with
  the file's name in it, `--dsds-control-md` tall with a `filled-neutral` Remove
  at `2xs` sitting in it — the design says the file is an answer already given.
  That is `.import-chosen` in `style.css` now and no longer anything this page
  asks for: the three sibling imports were drawn as a taller row with a link in
  them, the designer has settled all four on the field, and this frame is the one
  they were unified to. Only the wording and the behaviour still differ — Remove
  brings the dropzone back here, where the siblings' Choose another file reopens
  the picker. And the SOI's name on a card is *not* `soiChip`, the chip the SOIs
  table and the automations page draw an SOI as: the designer took that chip's
  border and inline padding off and set the name in `sm` semibold instead, keeping
  only the 2px above and below that holds the 20px identity symbol on a 24px line.
  `soiChip` is untouched, both of its callers being lists where a chip is right.
  `.ai/pr-notes/soi-import-audit.md` has the whole measurement, Paper against the
  running page, and the places where the design and the design system disagreed.

  All of it was driven against the sandbox: the list's order and
  tick states, the chip's count, Select all in each of its three states with the
  two counts agreeing, the three refusals, the file-level failure, in-file
  de-duplication, the strip in all three shapes and the empty-logic rejection, the
  uuid surviving an overwrite with its automation still resolving, e and f landing
  as "Name (2)" under uuids the sensor minted, the count on Import tracking the
  ticks, and the toast. Every fixture was built out of the sensor's own SOIs, so
  the logic sent is logic the sensor accepts, and the sensor was put back as it
  was found — including the SOI deliberately overwritten.

  **Every string on the tab says "waveform model"; nothing under it does.** The
  tab bar, the first column of both tables, the search placeholder, the Import
  button, the import dialog, the empty state, the toasts, the delete
  confirmation and the Last updated column's tooltip were renamed together; the
  SOIs column's tooltip was renamed with them and then went with the column. The
  ids, the CSS classes (`.detector-*`), the `data-testid`s, the URL hash
  (`#detectors`), `widgets/classifiers.ts`, the Go identifiers and the `xgboost`
  endpoints all still say detector or classifier. Don't rename them: they are
  invisible, and moving them touches the API, the config file on the sensor and
  every test that names one.

  Two things a user can still reach say "detector", both by decision rather than
  by oversight. The SOI editor one tab away — its variable dropdown's "Waveform
  detectors" `<optgroup>`, its threshold help, the SOIs import hint and the SOI
  import result's "won't match until the detector is installed" — is left until
  the modulation types are settled. And the sensor's own 409, below.

  The algorithm is named nowhere a user reads. It used to be in the import
  dropzone's second line, which now says what kind of file it wants without
  saying what trained it; the page's visible text was checked for "xgboost" and
  holds it nowhere, which is a cheap way to keep it out.

  Both tabs are a list you search and act on, so both are the same shape: a
  sticky bar over the table holding a search field on the left and the actions
  on the right, all at the design system's medium control height, with no row
  gap between the bar and the table — the bar's own bottom padding is what
  covers a row sliding under it. The rules are one set, named `.library-*` in
  `style.css` rather than `.sois-*`, since a second copy under a second name
  would be two ideas about what the bar is. The waveform models page has one
  action, Import waveform model, in the primary slot the SOIs page gives Create
  new: adding one is uploading a model file, so there is nothing to create
  from nothing and no second button. Its search matches the model's name and
  nothing else — the only other column is a date, and the names a model
  files its score under are not in the table, so a row matching one would look
  like a fault. Nothing on the sensor and nothing matching what was typed are
  different rows saying different things, the first a tab waiting to be used and
  the second a query to clear.

  A waveform model is trained to answer one question, so it scores one thing,
  and the portal names that score after the model: adding one asks for a name and
  a model file, and posts `outputs: [{index: 0, name}]` derived from the name. The
  name is lowercased with underscores for that reason — it is the variable an
  SOI's logic tests, and "Cell tower" would be tested as `{"var": "Cell tower"}`.
  The sensor's config can map several names onto one model, for a model that
  scores more than one class, and `handlers/logic_variables.go` honours that; the
  portal offers no way to set it up, because nothing that trains a waveform model
  produces one. The variable list also carries the models processing runs for
  itself, from `parameter_selector.json`. That is the wideband/narrowband
  selector and the radar detector behind the PDW gate. Processing files their
  scores into `custom_classifier_probs` through the same code as the configured
  ones, so an SOI can test them. They are flagged `builtin` because there is no
  entry to delete and no file to download. The Radar class locks to the built-in
  `radar` (`RADAR_MODEL`). A release config therefore has no reason to carry the
  same model in `custom_classifiers.json` too. The bright_loon configs did, and
  processing scored it twice. Adding a detector under a built-in's name is
  refused like any other collision (`builtinNameTaken`). The frontend cannot ask
  that as the name is typed, since `GET /api/v1/model/xgboost/config` lists the
  configured models only. So the table holds the name, when its model file arrived and the
  actions, and nothing else — no second column repeating the name back as a
  score, and no model file path, which is on the sensor and not something anyone
  acts on from here. There was a fourth column, counting the SOIs that test each
  model, and the designer took it out; what it counted is still counted, for the
  delete confirmation, which the note below sets out at length because the
  helpers behind it read as dead code to anyone who only sees that the column has
  gone. The first column is headed Waveform model, and so is the column the SOIs
  table one tab away gives them: the two hold different things — a model, and a
  reference to one — but they hold the same kind of thing, and heading them
  differently was making a distinction out of a difference nobody had asked
  about. Both were renamed at once for that reason, which is why one hunk of this
  change belongs to the SOIs page rather than to this one.

  The three columns are a third of the table each, held there by
  `table-layout: fixed` on `.library-table-thirds`, since in the auto layout a
  percentage is a hint the content can outvote. Equal quarters were the
  designer's call while there were four, and equal thirds is that intent carried
  forward rather than a fresh decision. The rule sets `box-sizing` beside the
  width because style.css resets it to border-box for anything carrying a
  `dsds-` class, which the two content headings do and the Actions one —
  `.ds-table-actions` and nothing else — does not; left to the browser's default
  that column reads its share as that share plus the cell's own padding and
  comes out wider than the ones it matches, which is what it did at quarters and
  what it would do again. The trap did not go away with the fourth column, so
  the three rendered widths were measured rather than trusted from the
  percentage — 325px each at a 1280px viewport. The same rule overrides
  `.ds-table-actions`' shrink-to-content width, which is right for every other
  table in the portal, and does it on class-plus-element because a page's
  `<style>` block sits above style.css in the cascade and so loses every tie to
  it. A fixed column does not grow for its content, so the name cell truncates
  with the whole name on its title, as the SOIs table's name column does.

  Actions at a third holds two buttons in 325px and is emptier than it was at a
  quarter, which is the cost of the decision rather than a fault in it: the
  buttons are right-aligned against the panel edge, there is no rule drawn
  between columns, and so what the reader sees is where Last updated sits and
  not how much room Actions was given. The alternative — the two content columns
  equal and Actions at the 92px its buttons ask for — was rendered for
  comparison and moves the emptiness rather than removing it: the content
  columns go to 441px each and the date moves 92px further from the name, which
  is the pair anyone reads across. Thirds keep them closer, so thirds are what
  is committed.

  That date is the model file's own modification time, stat'd by
  `GetXGBoostConfig` and returned beside each entry as `file_modified_s`, so the
  tab needs no second request and nothing new is written to the sensor.
  `custom_classifiers.json` records no date and is not the portal's to extend —
  the processing container reads it, `sandbox/capture.sh` copies it off real
  sensors, and the detection bundle's `CustomClassifier` mirrors its shape — so
  the listed form is a type of its own (`XGBoostModelListing`), which keeps the
  field out of the write path by construction. Two things follow. The column is
  called Last updated rather than Added, because an mtime moves: a file replaced
  on the sensor by hand dates from the replacement, and an entry whose file is
  gone has no date at all, showing a dash. And every detector already on a
  sensor has a date, which a field written at POST time would only have given
  the ones added after it. What a heading cannot say, the titles do — the
  column's own says the date is the model file's and that replacing the file
  moves it while renaming the detector does not, since the file it points at did
  not change, and each cell's repeats it beside the moment itself. The sensor
  overview page heads heartbeat freshness "Last updated" too; the same phrase
  for two different subjects is accepted rather than worked around, each page
  being read on its own. The wording — "3 days ago", with the moment on the
  title — is `relativeTime` in `widgets/automation_common.ts`, shared with the
  mission profiles page's last active time so the two pages date things alike.

  The cell begins with that wording rather than continuing a sentence with it, so
  the first letter is capitalised — "Today", not "today". That happens at this
  call site and not in `relativeTime`, because the shared helper's other callers
  put the same words mid-sentence ("Last active today" on mission profiles) where
  a capital would read as a mistake; a value already starting with a digit ("3
  days ago") comes back unchanged either way. The column reads at `fg-base` and
  not muted: when a model file was last replaced is the kind of thing somebody
  came to this tab to find out, not a caption on the name. The dash an entry with
  no file gets is still muted, that being an absence rather than an answer.

  That asking is a dialog opened from the bar and not a form under the table,
  which is what it was on the model page: the form was the last thing on the
  page whether or not anyone wanted it, and its own "Add a detector" heading was
  a second, quieter answer to the question the button now asks. The dialog is
  `ds-dialog`, like the mission profiles and automations transfers, and it is
  headed with the words on the button that opens it. Its description says what
  the dialog does — "Add a waveform model by importing it as a JSON file." — and
  not what a waveform model is: the page's own subhead says that over both tabs,
  to everybody, rather than only to whoever opened this. The one thing the
  paragraph that used to sit over the table said which is neither — that nothing
  is live until the processing service restarts — is the restart notice's to
  say, and the notice says it at the moment it applies with the restart on offer
  beside it, which a standing sentence never did.

  The file is asked for by the portal's one dropzone (`.import-drop`,
  `widgets/file_drop.ts`), the same box the automations and mission profiles
  imports use, down to the Tabler file-upload icon, the `--dsds-border-strong`
  dash and the two lines that are one weight apart in colour only — `fg-base`
  over `fg-muted` — see `design-system-components.md`. It was a native input
  dressed in the design system's border until those rules were promoted out of
  `automations.html`; the dropzone's second line is where the kind of file gets
  named, and deliberately not what trained it.

  Nothing stands under the name field until there is something to say about the
  name. The sentence that used to explain what the name was for whatever was
  typed has gone: the field is one of two in a dialog whose description already
  says what it is doing, and a line that never changes is a line nobody reads by
  the second visit. What replaces it is the same element saying what the typed
  name will be saved as, or which model already holds it, and collapsing when
  neither applies.

  Since the name is the variable, the dialog refuses one another waveform model
  already scores under, saying which model holds it, as you type and again
  against a fresh read of the config before the file is uploaded. Which
  model and why is the hint's, under the field the answer has to change in;
  the line above the footer says only that nothing was imported, since one
  refusal said twice in one dialog reads as two problems. A refusal never closes
  the dialog — the name that was typed and the file that was chosen are both
  still in it, and every way this fails is about the name. The backend refuses
  the same thing, because that is the case that matters: two scores filed under
  one name land on the same variable, one writes over the other, and every SOI
  testing it is scored by whichever model the processing service loaded last.
  Add asks it through `classifierNameTaken`, of every name the new entry would
  score under and not only its `modelname`, since it is a score landing on a
  taken variable that does the damage. A duplicate `modelname`
  keeps its own older 409 ("Classifier already exists") on the add path, checked
  first, so anything already reading that message still gets it.

  **The sensor's two collision messages still say "detector"** — "A detector
  called %s already exists." and "%s already scores under the name %s, and two
  detectors cannot share one." (`handlers/model.go`) — where the tab that shows
  them says "waveform model". Left deliberately, the designer being content for
  the server to be a word behind, but it is reachable rather than dead copy: the
  dialog refuses first from what it has listed, and the server re-checks under a
  lock, so two people importing the same name at once means the second reads the
  old noun. That refusal was asked of the API directly, and its shape checked
  rather than the noun in it, so the finding will not go stale when somebody does
  change the Go.

  Only writes are
  refused: a config the portal did not write can already hold the collision —
  the sandbox's has `wifi` and `wifi2` both scoring as `wifi` — and those
  entries go on listing, downloading and deleting as before, since refusing to
  read them would leave nobody able to clear the collision up.

  **There is no rename**, and its absence is a decision rather than a gap
  waiting to be filled. A detector's name is currently its identity: the config
  entry's `modelname`, the name its output files a score under, and the variable
  every SOI's detection logic tests are one string in three places, so renaming
  one has to rewrite every SOI that tests it — a write to two stores, one of them
  an unacknowledged ZMQ command, to change a label. That is deferred until models
  carry an id independent of their name, because the whole approach wants
  redesigning when they do: SOIs would reference the id, and a rename would touch
  one field in one file. A `PATCH .../xgboost/config/:model_name` doing the
  three-place rewrite existed on this branch and was removed rather than left
  unexposed — an endpoint that rewrites user data with no caller is worse than no
  endpoint — and is recoverable from git history or
  `phoebe/detection-library-wip-backup` when the engineers want it. Until then a
  detector is renamed by editing `custom_classifiers.json` on the sensor, which
  is also what has to happen to fix a config the portal refuses to write.

  Deleting is the one action that takes a detector's name away from the SOIs that
  test it, so its confirmation says what that does before it happens: it names
  them, caps the list at five with "and N more", and says plainly when none test
  it instead of showing an empty list. It reads the SOIs from `/api/v1/soi` and
  picks them out client-side, on `soisTesting` in
  `widgets/automation_common.ts`, which is where anything that parses detection
  logic belongs; an endpoint whose only job was to count SOIs would be a route
  earning its keep once per dialog. Nothing on the Go side answers "which SOIs
  test this detector".

  **A column used to answer the same question in every row, and its removal did
  not take the counting with it.** There was an SOIs column — the count, muted at
  zero, a dash where the sensor could not be asked, and the names on the cell's
  title — and the designer took it out, leaving three columns. The delete
  confirmation is unchanged and still wanted, so `affectedSois` and everything
  under it (`soisTesting`, `scoredAs`, `listNames`, `soisTestingPhrase`) are
  live code with one caller instead of two. **Don't delete them as dead just
  because no column calls them any more**; the tab has one place left that names
  the SOIs and that is the dialog nobody sees until they press Delete. The
  reading moved with them: `/api/v1/soi` is no longer fetched alongside the
  config on every refresh, since the only thing that needs it is a dialog that
  reads fresh when it opens.

  What counts as depending on a detector is every name its scores are filed
  under, not its `modelname`: the score name is what an SOI's logic actually
  tests. Ordinarily they are the same name and the distinction never arises, but a
  config the portal did not write can file the score elsewhere — the sandbox's
  `wifi2` scores as `wifi`, `pulse_radar2` as `radar` — and counting by the entry's
  own name warned about nothing for detectors plainly in use, the string "wifi2"
  appearing in no SOI at all. `affectedSois` in `widgets/classifiers.ts` is the one
  definition, asking `soisTesting` once per score name and keeping the SOI list's
  own order, so an SOI testing two outputs of one entry is affected once. Two
  entries scoring under one name — the collision the portal refuses to create and
  still reads — both warn about the SOIs testing it, which is what the config
  says. An SOI left testing a name no detector scores under any more is named
  against nothing at all, which is the honest answer rather than a case to
  handle. The delete dialog was opened on all three of the sandbox's legacy
  entries for exactly this, that being the coverage the column's removal left
  standing.

  Naming the detector in those sentences would name something no SOI mentions, so
  they name the variable and say whose it is once, in brackets: "1 SOI tests wifi
  (the name wifi2 scores under) and will stop matching". `scoredAs` produces the
  bare name where it is the entry's own, which is the overwhelming case, so the
  ordinary sentence is unchanged.

  **The tabs no longer tell each other anything.** `initClassifiers` returned a
  `refresh` that `refreshSoon` in `soi.ts` called after every SOI save, delete
  and import, because writing an SOI on that tab changed what the count on this
  one said. The count was the whole of the reason: a name and a date are the
  config's, not the SOIs', and the delete dialog reads the SOIs when it opens, so
  the controller, the field holding it and the calls went with the column rather
  than being left as a redraw nothing needs. `initClassifiers` returns `void`.
  There was never traffic the other way — nothing on this page moves a variable
  an SOI tests, now that there is no rename — so the SOIs page has nothing to be
  told either.

  A row's actions are one visible button and a `⋮` for the rest, which is the
  shape the SOIs table and the mission profiles list already have. Download is
  the button: it is the only way to get a model file back off the sensor, and the
  action that changes nothing. Delete is the menu's only item, the rename that
  used to sit above it having gone; it stays in the menu rather than returning to
  the row because it is the destructive action, and a delete button next to
  download in every row is the mis-click this shape exists to prevent. The menu
  is the existing
  `ds-menu` / `ds-menu-button` pair wired by `attachMenu`, which is what keeps it
  out of trouble: the wrap around the table scrolls, and a box positioned by the
  stylesheet inside a scrolling ancestor is a box that gets clipped, so the widget
  pins it to the viewport instead. On this page the last row always has more room
  under it than the menu is tall — the panel's own bottom padding — so the menu
  never has to flip above its trigger, which was checked on the last row of a
  30-row table with the panel scrolled, hit-testing each item rather than only
  measuring the box.

  Both halves of the library share one subhead, "Reusable signal definitions and
  AI models that recognize specific waveforms.", in the page header outside
  either panel: the library is one thing with two halves, and switching tabs does
  not change what it is. It is the sentence that says what a waveform model is,
  which is why the import dialog's description no longer tries to — a gloss over
  the whole page reaches everyone who came to look, and a dialog only reaches
  whoever opened it. American spelling, "recognize", which is what the page
  already uses.

  Where an SOI sets a detector's threshold, the logic builder gives it a slider
  in per cent instead of the number field the other variables get — the score is
  a confidence, and choosing one is a judgement about how much noise to accept.
  The logic still stores the 0-to-1 figure, and the slider is unstepped so a
  threshold written elsewhere survives being looked at. Asking for a window
  (`between`) puts the number fields back: that is two values, and this slider
  has one thumb.

  A menu inside a card needs the card told not to look pressed when one of the
  menu's own rows is pressed. The menu is a DOM descendant of the card, so
  `:active` lands on both, and the design system's pressed card carries an
  opacity — which makes the card a stacking context and traps the open menu's
  `z-index` inside it. The next card in the list then paints over the menu for as
  long as the pointer is down, so the press starts on the row, ends on the card
  below, and the click is delivered to their common ancestor. The row's action
  never runs and nothing appears, which is what "the menu does nothing" was:
  `.dsds-card:has(.ds-menu-anchor.expanded):active { opacity: 1 }` in
  `style.css`. Only rows overhanging the next card were affected, so the last
  card in a list and the lone active profile always worked — which is why this
  survived a green suite for months. It shipped with PR #177 and was found on the
  mission profiles list, where Delete was dead on every card but the last.

  Two things follow for checking this kind of thing. A menu row has to be clicked
  with a real pointer at its own pixels: an element click refuses with
  "intercepts pointer events", which fails for the wrong reason, and a JavaScript
  `.click()` dispatches straight to the node and passes however the page is
  painted. And the card under test has to
  have another card below it, since the ones that do not were never broken.
- **The models pages show the model manifest and write the overlay.** `GET /api/v1/model/manifest` (`handlers/ml_manifest.go`) reads
  `ml_manifest.json` (shipped, written when models are installed) and
  `ml_manifest.overlay.json` (the portal's, field models in its own
  `embedding_models[]`, `xgboost_models[]` and `mahalanobis_models[]`, and `{version_string, enabled}`
  in `overrides[]`) from the `-xgboost` directory. A Mahalanobis anomaly
  model is a child of its engine like a classifier (`<engine>/<name>`) and
  always scores under `anomaly_score` and `anomaly_cdf`, so the merge keeps
  one on per family; its file goes through `POST /api/v1/model/mahalanobis/upload`
  (`-anomaly_models`, `[paths] anomaly_model_upload_dir`), which refuses a
  file processing's loader would refuse (`handlers/mahalanobis_model.go`),
  and the entry through `POST /api/v1/model/manifest/anomaly_models`, which
  also checks the file's embedding width against the engine's signature.
  The model file is proto3 JSON of `ml_manifest/mahalanobis_model.proto`'s
  `MahalanobisModelFile` (bands with flat row-major matrices, plus the
  provenance of the fit: collections, sensor, frequencies, engine), or the
  bare band list older fitters wrote; the add copies the short form of the
  provenance onto the entry (`MahalanobisModel.fit`: when, by what and from
  how much it was fitted, and the span it covers), an entry with no engine
  takes the one the provenance names, and one naming another engine is
  refused. The whole feature sits behind
  `"anomaly_models": { "enabled": true }` in `config.json`
  (`AnomalyModelsFeatureFlags`, off by default): with it off the two routes
  are not registered, the page has no import button or dialog, and the
  table leaves the manifest's anomaly entries out. The waveform models
  table lists them in line with the XGBoost models, under the same engine
  headers and after the waveform models of the family, each row marked by
  a filled warning chip reading "Mahalanobis anomaly model" and painted
  like its neighbours; `widgets/anomaly_models.ts` builds those rows and
  owns the second import button and its dialog, and
  `widgets/manifest_models.ts` places them. Both files are proto3 JSON
  of `ml_manifest/ml_manifest.proto` in the schemas submodule, parsed with
  `protojson` (strict first, then `DiscardUnknown` with a warning naming the
  field) and written with `UseProtoNames`, so the shape is reviewed in one
  place and an empty list is left out of the file. The API is the schema's
  too: `ml_manifest/ml_manifest_api.proto` defines `ManifestView` (one
  `ModelReport` per entry, the file's own message plus `LoadStatus`, source,
  reason, `plan_trt` and `missing_files`), `Platform`, and the add, enable,
  choose-engine and pre-flight messages. The handlers serve them with
  `respondManifest` (proto names, unpopulated fields emitted) and read them
  with `bindManifest`, and the pages consume the generated TypeScript in
  `static/scripts/generated/ml_manifest/`. Only the error bodies stay the
  portal's `{"error": ...}`. The merge works on an internal `manifestEntry`
  that wraps the file message, so nothing is restated. The portal then
  merges them by the rules processing uses (ds
  `containers/processing/ProcessUtils/MLManifest.cc`), and returns one row per
  model with a `source` of shipped or field and an `expected_status`. The two
  merges have to stay identical, `manifest_uuid` included:
  `TestManifestUUIDMatchesProcessing` pins the Go value against the one the
  C++ test prints for the same document, so a change to either side fails a
  test on the other. The status is named `expected_status` because it is the
  status processing will give the entry at its next start, worked out from
  the files. The hash check and the loaders can still move an entry, and the
  loaded-models report carries the status they settled on: `ProcStatus.models`
  (one `ModelReport` per entry) rides along in the view as `reported[]` with
  `heartbeat_manifest_uuid`. The card calls the files
  applied when that uuid matches the merged one (falling back to
  `model_name` for an older processing), and then shows the reported status
  over the predicted one. A reported load failure is an error on the card.
  Wording follows the source: a row processing confirmed reads Running or
  Loaded, a row the files only predict reads Runs or Loads (`statusChip`'s
  `confirmed` flag, from `statusFor`). Eric, 2026-10-07: never say "loads"
  for something that is in fact loaded.

  **An engine is checked against the sensor before it is chosen.** A TensorRT
  plan only loads on the TensorRT version that wrote it and the GPU it was
  built on. `handlers/ml_platform.go` reads what this sensor is the way the
  portal can, from the files processing's runtimes come from: TensorRT from
  the `libnvinfer.so.<version>` soname, CUDA from `libcudart.so.<version>`
  trimmed to major.minor (the shape `cudaRuntimeGetVersion` gives processing),
  the SoC from the last entry of `/proc/device-tree/compatible`
  (`tegra234` on an Orin). `ManifestPlatformProbe` is a variable so tests set
  the platform. On a developer machine it reads nothing and the rule is off.
  The merge holds every engine to its `target {trt, cuda, soc}` and to the
  TensorRT version in its plan file's header (`planTRTVersion`: TensorRT 10
  writes it at byte 24, a TensorRT 8 plan has no header and reads as "8"),
  with processing's wording from `cannot_run` in ds, as `unsupported_platform`.
  The two legacy engine endpoints run the same header check: a `.trt` upload
  another TensorRT wrote is deleted and refused (400), and `POST
  /api/v1/model/engine` refuses (409) before touching config.ini. The target is
  not part of `manifest_uuid`. The sensor-setup gate this replaces matched
  the S3 object metadata (`device`, `nv-tegra-release`) by hand-written
  inventory values. The Yocto image has no `/etc/nv_tegra_release` and its
  device-tree model names the carrier, so neither value was readable on it.

  Uploads carry their manifest entry. `POST .../manifest/models` takes the
  classifier's `XgboostModel` (its file already uploaded through the xgboost
  upload endpoint, named in `files[0].path`) and `POST .../manifest/engines`
  takes an `EmbeddingModel` for a `.trt` uploaded through
  `/api/v1/model/upload`, which returns its path. The sensor fills the
  hashes and, for an engine, the TensorRT from the plan header, the CUDA and
  SoC it was accepted on and the running engine's contract and output layout
  (`embedding_signature`, there is no sidecar `.json` any more). A user
  supplies a name, an alias and the engine file. A field engine is installed, not chosen,
  and can be removed unless it is the chosen one. With a manifest the
  embedding models page is the manifest alone (the engine files table is the
  no-manifest fallback), and `PostActiveEngine`, `AddXGBoostClassifier` and
  `DeleteXGBoostClassifier` refuse with 409 (`manifestPresent`): config.ini
  and custom_classifiers.json are not read then, so a write to them is drift.
  Field rows read "User provided".

  A model is on or off and nothing else is chosen. There is no Replace: a
  model is imported with the output it scores under (the dialog's Output
  field, defaulting to the name) and arrives off when another on model of
  the running family already produces that output. Turn on switches to it,
  turning the other producer off in the same write, and Turn on on the
  shipped one switches back. `SUPERSEDED` is only ever a hand-edited file
  with two on producers, where the first in file order wins. There is no
  Reset to shipped (Eric, 2026-10-08: overloaded). A shipped model turned
  off here is an `enabled:false` override that Turn on flips, and
  `DELETE .../manifest/overrides` stays as the API way to drop an override.
  Required-ness lives in processing (`required_outputs` in the heartbeat):
  a write that would leave `wideband` or `radar` with no loaded producer is
  refused with 409. Eric, 2026-10-07: Replace was the wrong idea. Upload with
  the output set, then switch between valid required models.

  Writes (`handlers/ml_manifest_write.go`) follow the design's one rule: a
  shipped `version_string` may have `enabled` set and nothing else, as an
  override, and a required one refuses even that. A field `version_string` may
  be added, edited or deleted whole. One producing an output another model
  already produces is accepted and arrives superseded. The engine is chosen through
  `POST .../manifest/engine`, which runs the pre-flight first: refused (409)
  when the target family cannot run, otherwise the lost variables, the SOIs
  testing them and the models that stop, applied only with `confirm` when
  anything is lost. Choosing the shipped default removes the pin rather than
  adding one. Every write takes `manifestOverlayLock` and writes
  temp-and-rename like `custom_classifiers.json` before it. Reads take the
  lock too, for the in-place fallback.

  **`@` belongs to the engine, and a classifier is `<engine>/<name>`.** An
  engine's identity is `name@alias`, the MLflow registered model and its
  registry label, the meaning `@` already has there. A classifier is named by
  its family and what it does inside it, with its run and hash as provenance
  fields. One with no engine recorded is `unassigned/<name>`. The file holds
  `engine` and `name` only and the portal never writes the joined form. The
  API returns it as `version_string`, and the table shows the name with the
  engine in its own column. The identity carries a slash, so the per-model
  endpoints take `?version_string=` rather than a path segment. Replacing a shipped model keeps its outputs and asks
  for the new model's own name, which cannot be the replaced one's.

  A rule is only enforced where the manifest fills it in: a model with no
  `engine` recorded runs with whichever engine runs, an engine with no
  `contract` is runnable, and the required-roles check is off until some
  entry declares `required`. The import dialog offers the running engine
  first and "Not recorded" as the way to leave the rule off.

  The waveform models page is the manifest table when the sensor has a
  manifest (`widgets/manifest_models.ts`: rows grouped under one header per
  engine family, the running family first, with the Embedding models page's
  Set active on a family that is installed but not running
  (`widgets/engine_switch.ts` is the one switch flow both pages share),
  Download in the row, Turn on or Turn off and, on a field row, Delete behind
  the ⋮, and every write is a dry run shown as a change list first) and the
  older `custom_classifiers.json` page (`widgets/classifiers.ts`) when it has
  none. `waveform_models.ts` picks one after one read, so the shared import
  dialog has one owner. Both model pages
  carry Export manifest, which downloads the two manifest files as the sensor
  has them in one zip and no model files (`GET /api/v1/model/manifest/export`,
  `ExportManifest`). The browser's cookie carries the login, as for the model
  file download. An info mark after every name opens a hover panel
  (`widgets/tooltip.ts`, the design system's tooltip surface placed by
  script so a table wrap cannot clip it) with what the columns leave out:
  the MLflow run name, id, experiment, run URL and artifact, the role,
  engine, outputs and build target, and each file with its sha256 and size
  (`modelFacts` in `widgets/manifest.ts`). Eric, 2026-10-07: the run name
  and the rest live in the file, and the hover is how they reach the page. The embedding models
  page lists the engines with Set active going through the manifest, and the
  file table keeps upload and delete. Logic variables come from the merged
  manifest's loaded entries when there is one, required ones as built-in.
- Collection and dwell downloads are plain `.tar`, not `.tar.gz`, so the
  handler can set an exact `Content-Length` from a zero-content dry run
  through the same `tar.Writer` (`tarSize` in `handlers/collections.go`) and
  the browser's own progress bar tells the truth with no pass over the data
  first. Never set a `Content-Length` you cannot guarantee: Go stops writing
  at the declared count (every dwell over ~1.3 MB used to arrive truncated
  under the old size estimate), and Chrome saves a short HTTP/2 body as a
  complete download. gzip stays for the log archive only, where text shrinks
  tenfold; IQ data shrinks by about a fifth and costs a full compression pass
  to size. `main.go` uses `handlers.Recovery()` rather than `gin.Default()`
  so a handler that cannot finish a body it has started can
  `panic(http.ErrAbortHandler)` and have net/http reset the stream.
- **The collections page is one list; the backend says what each row is.**
  `GetCollections` and `GetDwellCollections` set `Kind`
  (`KindRadioPlanRecording`, `KindAutomationCaptures`, `KindManualDwell`,
  `KindAutomationDwell`, `KindUnspecifiedDwell` in `handlers/collections.go`,
  each named for what the collection is) from the directory a collection
  was filed in, `TaskedBy` for the two automation kinds, and `Href`, the
  row's own address, which the page uses for download and delete and hands
  to `/api/v1/collection_overview` and `/api/v1/collection_insights` as
  `?collection=`. Those two take a query parameter rather than a path of
  their own because a dwell's address ends in a gin wildcard, which leaves
  no room for a segment after it. `resolveCollectionRef` in
  `handlers/collection_index.go` turns the Href back into a directory under
  the same checks the download route makes. The page's filter is a tab bar
  and the choice is the hash. The sort is in localStorage. The frontend
  never decides a kind from a path.
- **A download takes `?types=`** (data, meta, embed, collection, arrow) and
  the tar filter is applied in `writeTar` for both the size pass and the
  write, so `Content-Length` stays exact. No parameter means every file, as
  before. The dialog gets its counts and sizes from the overview.
- **Insights cost what reading sigmf-meta costs, so they run as one job at a
  time and are kept.** Measured on an Orin against ext4 on LUKS: a sweep
  plan writes about 15 captures a second (1 MiB data, 12 KB meta, 512 B
  embed each). With the page cache dropped, a full scan of a simulated
  hour (47,079 captures, 47 GB) took 15.7 s, about 0.33 ms per meta, and
  5.5 s warm; the sampled scan of it took 1.6 s cold. Parallel readers gain
  nothing. A directory of a million entries lists in a second and stats in
  seven, so the list walk never stats a collection's files. A six-hour
  recording whole is therefore about 100 s cold.
  The overview (`indexCollection`, one readdir and stat) is computed on
  every call. The analysis (`handlers/collection_insights.go`) is a job:
  `sample` reads one capture out of every stride of the time-ordered
  captures, at most `-insights_sample_captures` (5000, under two seconds
  cold), at an offset that a hash moves from stride to stride
  (`sampleOffset`): a fixed every-k-th read aliases with a sweep, whose
  frequencies repeat in order, and reads only some of them whenever k
  shares a factor with the number of steps. The page's charts of captures
  per frequency and per minute are flat on a sweep, so when the counts are
  even (within sampling noise) they draw detected signals per capture
  instead (`frequencyFigure`, `timelineFigure` in `collections.ts`), `full` reads
  every one behind a progress bar with abort, and `insightsRunner` holds the
  portal's one slot, answering 409 with the running scan's Href so the page
  can offer to abort it. A result is kept in sqlite.db (`collection_insights`,
  schema version 2) and served again until Recompute, on the assumption that
  a finished collection does not change. The page shows a notice while files
  are still arriving. `metaSummary` decodes only the fields the charts use, so the
  500-entry gain table in every meta is skipped. Nothing reads IQ. The list
  page's sizes go through `summarizeDir`, cached per directory on its mtime
  and entry count, so a finished recording is stat'd once.

## Dwell control

- **A dwell names its outputs, and the page sends them on every task.** `DwellCmd.outputs`
  (schemas `DwellOutputs`) carries two switches, `record_iq` and `psd`, from the two boxes
  under Outputs. The page always sends the message, both boxes off included: an empty
  message means "tune and keep nothing", where no message means "as before", which the
  sensor treats as record only. That is the shape the automation runner and any older
  caller send, so their dwells still land on disk. `ValidateDwellCmd` has no opinion on
  outputs, and `TestValidateDwellCmd` holds that.
- **A row is the average of everything captured in its period.** The sensor's `DwellPsd`
  transforms every block and averages them into one row per `dwell_psd_period_ms` of samples,
  and stamps `PSD.duration_ns` and `blocks_averaged` on it. The status line under the
  waterfall reads them out, so an operator can see that a row stands for 105 ms over 8
  blocks rather than one 13 ms snapshot. A sensor that sends neither (an older build) gets
  no coverage clause.
- **The waterfall is a WebSocket, not an SSE stream.** `GET /api/v1/dwell_control/psd/ws`
  (`GetDwellPsdSocket`, gorilla/websocket) upgrades and writes one protojson PSD per
  message from `dwellPsdBroadcast`, which `heartbeatLoop` feeds from the comm bus topic
  `dwell_psd_tx`. The controller keeps that topic's name through its relay, as it does
  `stitch_psd_tx`, so the survey's PSDs and a dwell's never reach each other's page.
  Nothing is cached: a page that opens mid-dwell draws from the next row. The handler's
  success path is the one streaming route a Go test can reach, since nothing on it touches
  ZMQ: `TestGetDwellPsdSocketSendsPublishedPsds` dials an `httptest` server and calls
  `handlers.PublishDwellPsd`, which is what the heartbeat loop calls.
- **`widgets/waterfall.ts` keeps the image.** One offscreen canvas of one pixel per bin per
  row, 320 rows, scrolled down a row per PSD, and a visible canvas that is the history
  scaled to the card's width. The ramp spans a floor (the row's 5th percentile) and a
  ceiling (its maximum), each eased toward the running value by a tenth per row, so a
  quiet band and a busy one both use the whole ramp. A change of band or bin count
  restarts the image. The page reconnects a closed socket itself, doubling the wait up to
  ten seconds: a browser retries an `EventSource` on its own but never a WebSocket. The card
  sits beside the form (`.tasking-layout-side`, a second grid column taking the rest of the
  row) so the spectrum stays in view while the next task is written. Dynamic tasking keeps
  its PSD card under the form.
- **Until stopped, and Stop.** Collect by gains Until stopped, which sends no sample count:
  the sensor runs the dwell until a task replaces it (its sample limit still ends one that
  records). `ValidateDwellCmd` no longer treats a zero count as a forgotten field. Stop is
  `POST /api/v1/dwell_control/stop` (`PostDwellStop`): a PLAN task for the plan the last
  heartbeat named, 409 when none has, so nothing on the page guesses a plan id.
- **The left card is two halves: what runs, and the next dwell.** The tinted band across
  the top (`.running-band`, bled to the card's edges) is the radio's state from the
  heartbeat with Stop beside it, on the page only while a dwell runs; the form under it,
  titled NEXT DWELL, is the draft. The form's sections are fieldsets whose legend breaks a
  rule (`.task-section`): Tune (center frequency and sample rate; decimals convert to whole
  Hz; the AGC box with the manual gain under it), Kind of dwell (a design system toggle
  group of three buttons, Record, LookBack, Watch, with a line under it saying what the
  chosen kind does; `#dwell_mode` carries the value as `data-value`), Watch (the live
  waterfall box), and Advanced settings (SDR, bandwidth, SDR channel, resume previous task)
  as a folded row on the same rule, not a box of its own. The kind settles how long the
  dwell runs and what is kept: a recorded collection runs for a time or a sample count
  (`collection_fields`, with the name it is filed under), every other kind sends no count
  and runs until stopped; watching the waterfall ticks the box and takes it out of reach;
  the lookback keeps a rolling window of samples for clips. The one button at the foot says
  what sending the draft does to the radio: Start dwell when it is on its plan, Apply when
  the draft is the running dwell with a switch flipped, Replace dwell when its tuning or
  kind differs, with the same in words beside it (`#send_note`). Three rules settle what
  the form holds: it opens as whatever the radio is doing (on its plan, the defaults; on a
  dwell, that dwell with its waterfall switch, seeded once from the first heartbeat and
  never over something already typed, `seedForm`); the Live waterfall box holds the
  operator's last click on it (`waterfallChosen`); Watch shows the box on and out of reach
  only while Watch is chosen, and leaving Watch puts it back, so clicking through the kinds
  never changes what the next dwell asks for. The note under the kind
  says what it does with the numbers as chips. The bandwidth follows the sample rate until set lower by hand and can
  never be set above it (`validateDwell` names the mistake). Numbers the page works out are
  green `ds-chip`s, the sample rate a blue one wherever it drives them, and sizes an amber
  one (`setParts`, which rebuilds an element from text and chips only when the words
  change). Spans of time read in the unit they fit (`duration`); sizes are 4 bytes a
  sample; every clock is the sensor's wall time in UTC, 24-hour, with the date.
- **The Radio card says what the radio is doing.** `pollStatus` reads `/api/v1/status` every
  two seconds (the SDR list is filled from the first answer) and `updateRadioNow` reads the
  chosen row as `SdrStatus.fromJSON`: on its plan, or the dwell it is parked on
  (`SdrStatus.dwell`, schemas 1227e69: center, rate, bandwidth, outputs, name, start) with
  what its IQ is for and how long it has run.
- **A clip of IQ out of the dwell lookback.** Dwell with lookback (`outputs.lookback`) keeps
  a rolling window of the dwell's samples on the sensor; the kind's line is how far back
  this sensor can look at the rate on the form, the spool size over the rate ("keeps a
  rolling [2 min 14 s] of samples at [20 MS/s]"), from `GET /api/v1/dwell_control/lookback` (`GetDwellLookback`,
  `ReadLookbackSettings`: the `[processing]` lookback keys with ds's defaults behind them).
  The same route does the disk check (`CheckLookbackDisk`: a spool file already reserved at
  its size, or that much free under `disk_path`) and answers `ready` and `reason`; not
  ready, or `max_bytes` 0, makes the choice an error the form names. It also carries what
  the sensor last said it holds (`held_from/to_wall_ns`, `held_sample_rate_hz`,
  `held_age_ms`, from the untagged `LOOKBACK_STATUS` the sensor publishes once a second,
  kept by `RecordLookbackHeld` off the heartbeat loop); the page polls it, so the Dwell
  lookback card is live for a dwell that keeps IQ without drawing a waterfall. The
  waterfall's rows carry the same range a second fresher and keep the say while they
  arrive. The LookBack card is on the page only while the LookBack is the kind chosen or
  the sensor holds IQ (`#lookback_card`); for the other kinds it is not there at all, the
  kind buttons being where it is chosen. While nothing is held its gray stage says the task
  is what comes next. The empty waterfall is the same gray stage with the image and its
  axes hidden until a row lands (`.waterfall[data-rows="0"]`), carrying a button that ticks
  the box.
  Live, the card shows how full the lookback is ("Buffered [1 min 12 s] of [2 min 14 s] at
  [20 MS/s]" and a `ds-progress-bar`), and the clip is a window of wall time in the From
  and To fields, UTC with the date: typed, filled by a quick pick (last 10 s, last 1 min, everything held),
  or by a drag down the image (rows are one pixel high, so the pointer's y is the row;
  rows the sensor no longer holds are dimmed). The fields are the one source and the others
  write into them, widened to whole seconds; `clipWindow` bounds them to what is held and
  to an hour (`LookbackSaveMaxDuration` on the server), and the line under them names the
  clip's length and size at the held rate, or what is wrong. Sending the next task empties
  them. Download IQ clip or Save to Collections posts `POST /api/v1/dwell_control/lookback_save`
  (`PostLookbackSave`) with the window as the sensor's wall time in ns; the handler
  registers a waiter by tag (`AwaitLookbackStatus`), publishes the `LOOKBACK_SAVE`, and
  answers with the `LookbackStatus` the heartbeat loop takes off the bus
  (`PublishLookbackStatus`): 200 when written, 409 with the sensor's reason and held range
  when refused, 504 after `LookbackSaveTimeout`. The sensor writes the clip as a SigMF pair
  under its stream root, as a manual dwell; a written clip's answer carries `href` and
  `download_path` (`LookbackSavedJSON`, `LookbackCollectionHref`), the recording's address
  under `/api/v1/dwell_collections/<origin>/<path>`, which is the row the Collections page
  lists, what GET streams as a tar archive (`?types=` narrows it), what DELETE removes, and
  what the overview and insights routes take as `?collection=`. Download clicks an anchor
  at that address, so the archive the browser saves is the pair the sensor keeps.
- **The sandbox streams rows for a dwell that asked.** `sandbox/fakesensor` acts on
  `SDR_TASK` DWELL commands only to the extent of `outputs.psd`: for the length the
  collection would take it publishes a `dwell_psd_tx` row every 100 ms, a drifting
  carrier and a burst over a -105 dBm floor in mW per bin, stamped as 100 ms over 8 blocks,
  which is what the real
  `DwellPsd` node sends when the gain table is loaded.

